const DECK = {"title":"IHUM 242 · Islamic Humanities","weeks":[{"id":"found","title":"Foundations","color":"#1F4E8C","soft":"rgba(31,78,140,0.11)"},{"id":"law","title":"Law & learning","color":"#138A7E","soft":"rgba(19,138,126,0.11)"},{"id":"hist","title":"Empires & history","color":"#C25A16","soft":"rgba(194,90,22,0.11)"},{"id":"arts","title":"Literature & art","color":"#6A4BA8","soft":"rgba(106,75,168,0.11)"},{"id":"modern","title":"Modern era","color":"#2E7D4F","soft":"rgba(46,125,79,0.11)"}],"cards":[{"id":"umma","n":1,"week":1,"term":"Umma","simple":"The community of Muslim believers as a whole, across tribe, ethnicity and place.","formula":"<i>umma</i> · community, people","more":["The Qur’an uses <i>umma</i> for a people with a shared religious calling. After the <b>hijra (622)</b> it came to mean the new community at Medina, bound by faith rather than by kinship, which was a sharp break with tribal Arabia. The early agreement known as the “Constitution of Medina” also brought allied Jewish clans within its terms of mutual defence.","<b>Common confusion:</b> the umma is a religious community, not a state. It has been ruled by caliphs, by many rival dynasties at once and today by dozens of nation-states. Modern movements from pan-Islamism to the Organisation of Islamic Cooperation (founded 1969) appeal to it as a transnational ideal.","It is also not an Arab community: most Muslims today are not Arabs, and a majority live in the Asia-Pacific region."],"connections":["hijra","pillars","caliph","sunnishia"],"example":"Indonesia has the largest Muslim population of any country. Its pilgrims at the hajj in Mecca, praying alongside Nigerians, Turks and Bosnians, are the umma made visible.","numbers":[["Muslims worldwide, 2020","≈ 2.0 billion"],["Share of world population, 2010","23.9%"],["Share of world population, 2020","25.6%"],["Growth, 2010–2020","+347 million"],["Shi‘i share of Muslims (approx.)","10–13%"]],"numbersSource":"Pew Research Center, “How the Global Religious Landscape Changed From 2010 to 2020” (2025); Pew, “Mapping the Global Muslim Population” (2009) for the Shi‘i range.","played":["622: the hijra founds a community at Medina defined by faith, not tribe.","632: Muhammad dies, and the umma must decide who leads it (the caliphate).","From the 10th century: one umma, many rulers. Rival caliphs and sultans govern, but scholars, pilgrims and trade keep a shared religious world.","Today: about a quarter of humanity, in dozens of states, still spoken of as one umma."],"graph":"ihum242Population","starred":false},{"id":"hijra","n":2,"week":1,"term":"Hijra (622 CE)","simple":"Muhammad’s emigration from Mecca to Medina in 622, which founded the first Muslim community-state and marks year 1 of the Islamic (AH) calendar.","formula":"1 AH = 622 CE · <i>hijra</i> · emigration","more":["After years of opposition from the leaders of Quraysh in Mecca, Muhammad accepted an invitation from the tribes of Yathrib, which became <i>Madinat al-Nabi</i>, “the city of the Prophet”. The emigrants (<i>muhajirun</i>) and the Medinan helpers (<i>ansar</i>) formed one community under his leadership.","The <b>Islamic calendar</b>, traditionally instituted under the caliph ‘Umar, counts years from the start of the year of the hijra (AH = <i>anno Hegirae</i>). Its year is twelve lunar months, so it runs about 11 days shorter than the solar year and Ramadan moves through the seasons.","<b>Common confusion:</b> the calendar does not start at Muhammad’s birth (traditionally 570) or the first revelation (610), but at the founding of the community."],"connections":["umma","quran","mosqueparts"],"example":"The Prophet’s Mosque in Medina began as the courtyard of Muhammad’s house after the hijra, the model for later congregational mosques.","numbers":[["First day of 1 AH","16 July 622 (Julian)"],["Islamic year","12 lunar months, ≈ 354 days"],["Drift against the solar year","≈ 11 days a year"]],"numbersSource":"Encyclopaedia Britannica, “Hijrah” and “Islamic calendar”.","played":["610: the first revelation, in Mecca (traditionally on Mount Hira).","622: the hijra to Medina; year 1 AH.","630: Mecca submits to Muhammad without major bloodshed.","632: Muhammad dies in Medina, now head of much of Arabia."],"graph":{"key":"ihum242Map","args":{"route":true,"hi":["Mecca","Medina"]}},"starred":false},{"id":"quran","n":3,"week":1,"term":"Qur’an","simple":"The scripture of Islam, 114 suras (chapters) in Arabic, which Muslims believe is God’s word revealed to Muhammad between about 610 and 632.","formula":"<i>qur’an</i> · recitation","more":["The suras are arranged roughly from longest to shortest after the short opening sura, <i>al-Fatiha</i>, not in the order of revelation. Scholars group them as <b>Meccan</b> (shorter, on God, judgement and prophecy) and <b>Medinan</b> (longer, including community law).","Muslim tradition holds that the text was gathered after Muhammad’s death and fixed in a standard codex under the caliph ‘Uthman (r. 644–656). Muslims regard the Arabic text as inimitable (<i>i‘jaz</i>), so translations are treated as interpretations. Its literary Arabic became the reference standard for the language and for later literature.","<b>Common confusion:</b> the Qur’an is not the hadith. The Qur’an is held to be God’s speech; hadith are reports about what the Prophet said and did."],"connections":["hadith","sharia","hijra","qasida","pillars"],"example":"<i>Al-Fatiha</i> (7 verses) is recited in every unit (<i>rak‘a</i>) of the daily prayer, so a Muslim who prays recites it at least 17 times a day.","numbers":[["Suras","114"],["Verses (Kufan count)","6,236"],["Period of revelation","c. 610–632"]],"numbersSource":"Encyclopaedia Britannica, “Qur’an”; verse total by the Kufan count used in the standard Cairo edition (other early counts differ slightly).","played":["c. 610–632: revealed piecemeal, recited and memorized.","After 632: collected into written form (tradition credits Abu Bakr and Zayd ibn Thabit).","‘Uthman (r. 644–656): a standard codex is copied and sent to the main cities.","1924: the Cairo printed edition becomes the most widely used text today."],"graph":{"key":"ihum242LawLadder","args":{"hi":"quran"}},"starred":false},{"id":"hadith","n":4,"week":1,"term":"Hadith & sunna","simple":"Hadith are reports, each carried by a chain of transmitters (isnad), of what Muhammad said, did or approved, and they are the main evidence for his sunna, the normative example Muslims follow.","formula":"hadith = <i>isnad</i> (chain) + <i>matn</i> (text)","more":["<b>Sunna</b> is the model; <b>hadith</b> are the evidence for it. Scholars judged a report mainly by its chain: were the transmitters reliable, and could they have met? Reports were graded <i>sahih</i> (sound), <i>hasan</i> (good) or <i>da‘if</i> (weak).","Sunnis give special authority to six 9th-century collections, above all those of al-Bukhari (d. 870) and Muslim (d. 875). Shi‘is rely on their own collections, such as al-Kulayni’s <i>al-Kafi</i>, which also include the sayings of the Imams.","<b>Common confusion:</b> a hadith is not part of the Qur’an, and “sunna” here is not the same word as “Sunni”, though Sunnis take their name from it (<i>ahl al-sunna</i>, “people of the sunna”)."],"connections":["quran","sharia","ulama","sufism","pillars"],"example":"The “Hadith of Gabriel” in Muslim’s <i>Sahih</i>: a stranger questions the Prophet about <i>islam</i>, <i>iman</i> (faith) and <i>ihsan</i> (excellence), then is identified as the angel Gabriel.","played":["A Companion sees or hears the Prophet and reports it.","Successive transmitters pass it on: “A told me that B said that…”.","A 9th-century compiler checks each link and grades the chain.","Jurists then use a sound hadith as the second source of law after the Qur’an."],"graph":"ihum242Isnad","starred":false},{"id":"pillars","n":5,"week":1,"term":"Five pillars","simple":"Shahada (profession of faith), salat (prayer five times a day), zakat (obligatory alms), sawm (fasting in Ramadan), hajj (pilgrimage to Mecca, once if able).","more":["The pillars are acts, not beliefs: the shared ritual core that makes Muslims across schools and sects recognizable to one another. Sunnis and Shi‘is perform all five, though Shi‘i theology classifies the obligations in its own way and adds others.","<b>Zakat</b> is not voluntary charity (<i>sadaqa</i>) but an obligation, customarily 2.5% of qualifying wealth held for a year. <b>Sawm</b> means no food, drink or sex from dawn to sunset during Ramadan, the ninth lunar month.","<b>Common confusion:</b> the hajj is the pilgrimage in the month of Dhu al-Hijja; a visit to Mecca at other times is the lesser pilgrimage, <i>‘umra</i>."],"connections":["umma","quran","hadith","waqf"],"example":"On 9 Dhu al-Hijja pilgrims stand in prayer on the plain of ‘Arafat; the next day Muslims everywhere celebrate ‘Id al-Adha with a sacrifice.","numbers":[["Hajj pilgrims, 2024 (1445 AH)","1,833,164"],["of whom from abroad","1,611,310"],["Daily prayers","5"],["Zakat, customary rate","2.5% of qualifying wealth"]],"numbersSource":"Saudi General Authority for Statistics (GASTAT), Hajj Statistics 2024; Encyclopaedia Britannica, “Pillars of Islam”.","played":["8 Dhu al-Hijja: pilgrims in <i>ihram</i> (simple white garments, a state of consecration) go to Mina.","9th: standing at ‘Arafat, the heart of the hajj.","10th: stoning of the pillar at Mina, animal sacrifice, cutting the hair.","Circling the Ka‘ba (<i>tawaf</i>) completes the rites."],"graph":{"key":"ihum242Pillars","args":{"hi":"hajj"}},"starred":false},{"id":"caliph","n":6,"week":1,"term":"Caliph / caliphate","simple":"The caliph (<i>khalifa</i>, “successor”) was the leader of the Muslim community after Muhammad: first the four Rashidun (632–661), then the Umayyad and Abbasid dynasties.","formula":"<i>khalifa</i> · successor, deputy","more":["A caliph succeeded to Muhammad’s leadership of the community, <b>not</b> to his prophethood: no new revelation. Early caliphs were styled “successor of the Messenger of God”; the Umayyads also used “deputy of God”, a claim to religious authority later scholars resisted.","After the four Rashidun caliphs the office became dynastic: Umayyads, then Abbasids. From the 10th century there were rival caliphs (Fatimids in Cairo, Umayyads in Córdoba). After the Mongols took Baghdad in 1258, the Mamluks kept a line of figurehead Abbasid caliphs in Cairo. The Ottoman caliphate was abolished in 1924.","<b>Common confusion:</b> “caliph” and “sultan” differ. Sultans held military power; caliphs (after c. 945) were often symbols of legitimacy who confirmed them."],"connections":["sunnishia","umayyad","abbasid","mamluk","umma"],"example":"Abu Bakr, Muhammad’s father-in-law and close Companion, was acclaimed the first caliph at a meeting in Medina (the Saqifa) in 632.","numbers":[["Abu Bakr","632–634"],["‘Umar","634–644"],["‘Uthman","644–656"],["‘Ali","656–661"],["Ottoman caliphate abolished","3 March 1924"]],"numbersSource":"Encyclopaedia Britannica, “caliph” and entries on each caliph.","played":["632: Abu Bakr chosen in Medina.","656: ‘Uthman is killed; ‘Ali’s caliphate is contested in the first civil war (fitna).","661: ‘Ali is assassinated; Mu‘awiya founds the Umayyad dynasty.","1924: the Turkish Republic abolishes the last caliphate."],"graph":"ihum242Succession","starred":false},{"id":"sunnishia","n":7,"week":1,"term":"Sunni–Shi‘i divide","simple":"The split between Sunnis and Shi‘is began as a dispute over who should lead after Muhammad, with Shi‘is (“partisans” of ‘Ali) holding that leadership belonged to ‘Ali and his descendants, the Imams.","formula":"<i>shi‘at ‘Ali</i> · the party of ‘Ali","more":["<b>Sunnis</b> (<i>ahl al-sunna wa-l-jama‘a</i>) accept the first four caliphs, in their historical order, as rightly guided, with leadership resting on the community’s choice. <b>Shi‘is</b> hold that Muhammad designated ‘Ali and that religious authority passed to a line of Imams from his family. Twelvers (the largest Shi‘i branch) believe the twelfth Imam went into hiding in 874 and will return.","Both share the Qur’an, the five pillars and prayer toward Mecca. They differ in hadith collections, schools of law (the Shi‘i Ja‘fari school), some ritual details and religious authority: in modern Twelver Shi‘ism, senior jurists act as guides for believers.","<b>Common confusion:</b> the divide was political before it was theological, and it is not simply Iran vs Arabs: there are large Shi‘i populations in Iraq, Pakistan, India and Lebanon."],"connections":["caliph","umayyad","gunpowder","umma"],"example":"At Karbala (680) the Prophet’s grandson Husayn and a small band were killed by an Umayyad army. Shi‘is mourn him every year on ‘Ashura, 10 Muharram, and his shrine at Karbala is a major pilgrimage site.","numbers":[["Shi‘i share of all Muslims","≈ 10–13%"],["Sunni share","≈ 87–90%"],["Shi‘is living in Iran, Pakistan, India, Iraq","68–80%"],["Karbala","10 Muharram 61 AH (10 Oct 680)"]],"numbersSource":"Pew Research Center, “Mapping the Global Muslim Population” (2009), estimates given as ranges because sectarian data are sparse; Encyclopaedia Britannica, “Battle of Karbala”.","played":["632: Abu Bakr becomes caliph; ‘Ali’s supporters believe he should have led.","656–661: ‘Ali is caliph amid civil war, then is killed.","680: Husayn is killed at Karbala, giving the movement its martyr.","1501: the Safavids make Twelver Shi‘ism the state religion of Iran."],"graph":"ihum242Succession","starred":false},{"id":"sharia","n":8,"week":2,"term":"Sharia vs fiqh","simple":"Sharia is God’s law as an ideal path, while fiqh is the human jurisprudence by which scholars derive concrete rulings from the Qur’an, sunna, consensus (ijma‘) and analogy (qiyas).","formula":"<i>shari‘a</i> · the path · <i>fiqh</i> · understanding","more":["Sharia is God’s law as an ideal; <b>fiqh</b> is the jurists’ fallible attempt to know it, which is why it is plural (several schools) and can change. The effort of reasoning is <i>ijtihad</i>; following an established school’s rulings is <i>taqlid</i>.","Fiqh sorts every act into five categories: obligatory, recommended, permitted, discouraged and forbidden. Much of it concerns ritual (prayer, fasting, purity), not only crime or the state.","<b>Common confusion:</b> “sharia law” in modern headlines usually means a state’s codified selection of fiqh, something premodern jurists, working through schools and judges, did not have."],"connections":["quran","hadith","ulama","madrasa","modernism"],"example":"The Qur’an forbids <i>khamr</i> (wine). By analogy (<i>qiyas</i>), jurists extended the ban to other intoxicants because they share its effective cause, intoxication.","played":["Question: is date-wine allowed? No explicit verse names it.","Qur’an: wine is forbidden. Sunna: hadith say “every intoxicant is forbidden”.","Cause (<i>‘illa</i>) of the rule: intoxication. Date-wine intoxicates.","Ruling by analogy: forbidden. Where scholars agree, consensus (<i>ijma‘</i>) settles it."],"graph":"ihum242LawLadder","starred":false},{"id":"ulama","n":9,"week":2,"term":"‘Ulama’","simple":"The ‘ulama’ are the learned scholars of religion and law (jurists, judges, teachers and muftis), who often mediated between rulers and the people.","formula":"<i>‘alim</i> (pl. <i>‘ulama’</i>) · one who knows","more":["Islam has no ordained clergy or church. Scholarly authority came from learning, certified person to person: a teacher gave a student an <i>ijaza</i> (licence) to transmit a text. Most Sunni scholars belonged to one of four schools of law (madhhabs).","A <b>qadi</b> judges cases in court; a <b>mufti</b> gives non-binding legal opinions (<i>fatwas</i>). Rulers needed the ‘ulama’ for legitimacy and administration; the ‘ulama’ needed patronage, often through waqf endowments.","<b>Common confusion:</b> in Twelver Shi‘ism a hierarchy of senior jurists (<i>mujtahids</i>, the highest a <i>marja‘</i>, “source of emulation”) developed, giving Shi‘i ‘ulama’ more independent authority than most Sunni scholars had."],"connections":["madrasa","sharia","waqf","modernism","hadith"],"example":"Ebussuud Efendi (d. 1574), chief mufti (<i>şeyhülislam</i>) under Süleyman the Magnificent, issued fatwas reconciling the sultan’s laws with the sharia.","played":["A student reads law with a master in a madrasa.","He receives an <i>ijaza</i>, a licence to teach that text.","He is appointed qadi, or teaches and issues fatwas as a mufti.","His rulings join his school’s tradition for later jurists."],"graph":"ihum242Madhhabs","starred":false},{"id":"madrasa","n":10,"week":2,"term":"Madrasa","simple":"A madrasa is a college of religious and legal learning, which spread widely from the 11th century, notably through the Seljuk vizier Nizam al-Mulk’s Nizamiyya colleges.","formula":"<i>madrasa</i> · place of study","more":["A madrasa taught fiqh above all, with hadith, Qur’an and Arabic, and usually lodged and paid its students. It was funded by a <b>waqf</b>, and its deed often fixed which school of law it served.","Nizam al-Mulk (d. 1092), vizier to the Seljuk sultans, founded madrasas in Baghdad and other cities. They trained Sunni scholars and officials at a time when the Shi‘i Fatimids ruled Egypt. Al-Ghazali taught at the Baghdad Nizamiyya.","<b>Common confusion:</b> a degree came from a teacher’s licence (<i>ijaza</i>), not from the institution. And the madrasa was not the first place of learning: teaching had long happened in mosques."],"connections":["waqf","ulama","sharia","mosqueparts","mamluk"],"example":"The Sultan Hasan complex in Cairo (1356–1363), built under the Mamluks, places four madrasas, one for each Sunni school of law, around a four-iwan courtyard.","numbers":[["Al-Azhar, Cairo (mosque, later a centre of learning)","970–972"],["Nizamiyya of Baghdad founded","1067"],["Sultan Hasan complex, Cairo","1356–1363"]],"numbersSource":"Encyclopaedia Britannica, “Niẓāmiyyah”, “al-Azhar University” and “Mosque of Sultan Ḥasan”.","played":["A founder endows shops or land as a waqf.","The income builds the madrasa and pays professors (Shafi‘i law at the Baghdad Nizamiyya).","Students live there on stipends and study for years.","Graduates become qadis, teachers and officials across the realm."],"graph":{"key":"ihum242MosquePlan","args":{"type":"iwan"}},"starred":false},{"id":"waqf","n":11,"week":2,"term":"Waqf","simple":"A charitable endowment: property whose income is dedicated in perpetuity to a purpose such as a mosque, madrasa, hospital or fountain.","formula":"<i>waqf</i> (pl. <i>awqaf</i>) · “stopping”, property held back","more":["The founder gives up ownership; the property can no longer be sold or inherited, and an administrator (<i>mutawalli</i>) under a judge’s supervision spends the income as the deed directs. It was the financial backbone of much of the premodern Islamic world’s education and welfare.","A <b>family waqf</b> could name the founder’s descendants as beneficiaries, which also protected wealth from confiscation and from the fixed shares of inheritance law.","<b>Common confusion:</b> waqf is not zakat. Zakat is an annual obligation on wealth; a waqf is a voluntary, permanent endowment. In the 19th and 20th centuries states took waqfs under ministries, weakening the ‘ulama’s independent income."],"connections":["madrasa","ulama","tanzimat","pillars"],"example":"The Süleymaniye complex in Istanbul ran on waqf income: besides the mosque it endowed madrasas, a hospital, a public kitchen (<i>imaret</i>) and baths.","played":["A merchant signs a deed before a qadi dedicating the rents of his shops.","The shops can never again be sold.","The administrator collects the rent and pays teachers and a fountain’s upkeep.","Centuries later, the Ottoman state places waqfs under a ministry (1826)."],"graph":"ihum242Waqf","starred":false},{"id":"dhimmi","n":12,"week":2,"term":"Dhimmi & jizya","simple":"Dhimmis were protected non-Muslim communities (Jews, Christians and others) under Muslim rule, who kept communal autonomy and paid a poll tax, the jizya.","formula":"<i>dhimma</i> · pact of protection","more":["The status rested on the Qur’an’s recognition of “People of the Book” and on surrender treaties made during the conquests; it was later extended to Zoroastrians and others. Dhimmis kept their worship, property and communal law, and paid the jizya in place of military service.","Juristic texts also imposed restrictions (on new church building, dress, bearing arms), often attributed to a “Pact of ‘Umar” whose early date historians doubt. How strictly they were enforced varied greatly by ruler and period.","<b>Common confusion:</b> dhimma was neither modern equality nor constant persecution; it was a protected but subordinate status. For centuries most people in conquered lands like Egypt and Syria were dhimmis."],"connections":["millet","tanzimat","devshirme"],"example":"Hasdai ibn Shaprut, a Jewish physician and diplomat, served the Umayyad caliph ‘Abd al-Rahman III in 10th-century Córdoba.","played":["7th century: a city surrenders by treaty; its Christians keep churches and pay tribute.","Jurists systematize the terms: jizya per adult male, communal self-rule.","Ottoman era: communities organized under their own religious heads (the millets).","1856: the Ottoman Reform Edict declares equality and ends the jizya."],"starred":false},{"id":"wisdom","n":13,"week":2,"term":"House of Wisdom & translation movement","simple":"In Abbasid Baghdad (8th–10th c.), Greek, Persian and Indian works in philosophy, medicine, mathematics and astronomy were translated into Arabic, often through Syriac.","formula":"<i>Bayt al-Hikma</i> · House of Wisdom","more":["Caliphs, viziers and wealthy patrons paid for translations for practical and prestige reasons: medicine, astronomy for the calendar and astrology, and arguments for theology. Cheap paper, which spread from the mid-8th century, made books far cheaper.","Many translators were Christians, such as <b>Hunayn ibn Ishaq</b> (809–873), who rendered Galen into Syriac and Arabic. The House of Wisdom under al-Ma’mun (r. 813–833) is famous, but historians debate whether it was an academy or mainly a palace library; the movement was broader than one building.","<b>Common confusion:</b> scholars writing in Arabic did not just preserve the Greeks for Europe. They criticized and extended them, and from the 12th century their own works were translated into Latin."],"connections":["falsafa","abbasid","adab"],"example":"Al-Khwarizmi (c. 780–c. 850), working in Baghdad, wrote <i>al-Jabr</i>, which gave us “algebra”; the Latin form of his name gave us “algorithm”.","numbers":[["Baghdad founded","762"],["Al-Ma’mun’s reign","813–833"],["Hunayn ibn Ishaq","809–873"]],"numbersSource":"Encyclopaedia Britannica, “Baghdad”, “al-Maʾmūn” and “Ḥunayn ibn Isḥāq”.","played":["Galen’s medical works, in Greek.","Hunayn’s circle translates them into Syriac and Arabic in 9th-century Baghdad.","Physicians like Ibn Sina (980–1037) build on them in Arabic.","12th century: Gerard of Cremona in Toledo translates Ibn Sina’s <i>Canon</i> into Latin for European medical schools."],"graph":"ihum242Translation","starred":false},{"id":"falsafa","n":14,"week":2,"term":"Falsafa vs kalam","simple":"Falsafa is philosophy in the Greek tradition (al-Kindi, al-Farabi, Ibn Sina, Ibn Rushd), while kalam is dialectical theology that uses reason to defend revealed doctrine (Mu‘tazilis, Ash‘aris).","more":["Both use reason; they differ in starting point. <b>Falsafa</b> starts from demonstrative reason and Greek texts and sees revelation as truth for the many in images. <b>Kalam</b> starts from revelation and uses argument to defend and clarify it.","The <b>Mu‘tazila</b> stressed God’s justice, human free will and the createdness of the Qur’an. The <b>Ash‘aris</b> (after al-Ash‘ari, d. 935/936) stressed God’s omnipotence, holding that God directly creates every event; this became the main Sunni theology alongside the Maturidi school.","<b>Common confusion:</b> al-Ghazali (1058–1111) did not reject reason or logic. His <i>Incoherence of the Philosophers</i> attacked three of their theses as unbelief: the world’s eternity, God’s not knowing particulars, and denial of bodily resurrection. Ibn Rushd (1126–1198) replied with the <i>Incoherence of the Incoherence</i>."],"connections":["wisdom","sufism","abbasid"],"example":"In the <i>mihna</i> (from 833) the caliph al-Ma’mun required scholars to affirm the Mu‘tazili doctrine that the Qur’an was created. Ahmad ibn Hanbal refused and was imprisoned; the policy was abandoned c. 848.","played":["Ibn Sina (980–1037) builds a great philosophical system in Arabic.","Al-Ghazali’s <i>Incoherence of the Philosophers</i> (c. 1095) attacks its key claims.","Ibn Rushd answers him (c. 1180), defending philosophy as compatible with revelation.","Ibn Rushd’s commentaries on Aristotle shape Latin scholasticism (“Averroes”)."],"graph":"ihum242FalsafaKalam","starred":false},{"id":"sufism","n":15,"week":2,"term":"Sufism (tasawwuf)","simple":"The mystical dimension of Islam: seeking direct experience of God through devotion, remembrance (dhikr) and discipline, often organized in orders (tariqas) around a master.","formula":"<i>tariqa</i> · path, order · <i>dhikr</i> · remembrance","more":["It grew from early ascetics such as Hasan al-Basri (d. 728) and the woman mystic Rabi‘a al-‘Adawiyya (traditionally d. 801), who spoke of loving God for Himself. From the 12th–13th centuries <b>orders</b> formed, each tracing its teaching through a chain of masters (<i>silsila</i>) back to the Prophet.","Al-Ghazali’s <i>Revival of the Religious Sciences</i> helped place a sharia-observant Sufism at the centre of Sunni life. Orders spread Islam in Anatolia, Central and South Asia and Africa, and some became political: the Safavid dynasty began as a Sufi order.","<b>Common confusion:</b> Sufism is not a sect beside Sunni and Shi‘i Islam; Sufis are found in both, and most followed the law. Some reformers since the 18th century have criticized practices such as saint veneration."],"connections":["ghazal","falsafa","hadith","gunpowder"],"example":"The Mevlevi order, founded by followers of Rumi (1207–1273) in Konya, performs the <i>sema</i>, the “whirling” ceremony of remembrance.","played":["A seeker (<i>murid</i>) takes an oath of allegiance to a shaykh.","He practises the order’s dhikr, often chanting God’s names, and spiritual discipline.","He passes through spiritual stations (<i>maqamat</i>) under guidance.","Authorized in turn, he becomes a link in the silsila."],"graph":{"key":"ihum242Isnad","args":{"mode":"silsila"}},"starred":false},{"id":"umayyad","n":16,"week":3,"term":"Umayyad caliphate (661–750)","simple":"The first hereditary caliphate, ruled from Damascus, which expanded from Iberia to Central Asia, made Arabic the language of government and built the Dome of the Rock.","more":["Founded by <b>Mu‘awiya</b> after ‘Ali’s death, it turned the caliphate into a hereditary monarchy, which critics, Shi‘i and later Sunni alike, condemned. It relied on an Arab military elite; non-Arab converts (<i>mawali</i>) resented unequal treatment, and that resentment fed the Abbasid revolution.","Under <b>‘Abd al-Malik</b> (r. 685–705) the state switched its records to Arabic, minted new coins bearing only inscriptions, and built the Dome of the Rock. The conquests reached Iberia (711) and Sind and Transoxiana; a raid into Francia was turned back at Tours (732).","<b>Common confusion:</b> the Umayyads of Córdoba are a separate branch: a survivor, ‘Abd al-Rahman I, founded an emirate there in 756, and ‘Abd al-Rahman III declared a rival caliphate in 929."],"connections":["abbasid","caliph","sunnishia","mosqueparts","arabesque"],"example":"The Great Mosque of Damascus, built under al-Walid I (r. 705–715) on the site of a Byzantine church, with mosaics of trees, rivers and buildings but no human figures.","numbers":[["Umayyad caliphate","661–750"],["Dome of the Rock completed","72 AH (691–692)"],["Conquest of Iberia begins","711"],["Umayyads of Córdoba","756–1031"]],"numbersSource":"Encyclopaedia Britannica, “Umayyad dynasty” and “Dome of the Rock”.","played":["661: Mu‘awiya takes power, capital Damascus.","680: Husayn is killed at Karbala by an Umayyad army.","691–692: the Dome of the Rock rises in Jerusalem.","750: the Abbasids overthrow the dynasty; 756: a survivor rules in Córdoba."],"graph":{"key":"ihum242Timeline","args":{"hi":["Umayyad","Umayyads of Córdoba"]}},"starred":false},{"id":"abbasid","n":17,"week":3,"term":"Abbasid caliphate (750–1258)","simple":"The dynasty that overthrew the Umayyads in 750, founded Baghdad in 762 and presided over a golden age of learning, though its real power fragmented long before the Mongols sacked Baghdad in 1258.","more":["The Abbasids claimed descent from al-‘Abbas, Muhammad’s uncle, and rallied Persian converts and supporters of the Prophet’s family under black banners. Once in power they ruled as Sunni caliphs, disappointing many Shi‘i hopes, and adopted Persian court and bureaucratic traditions, led by viziers.","Under Harun al-Rashid (r. 786–809) and al-Ma’mun (r. 813–833) Baghdad became a centre of trade, translation and scholarship. From 945 the Shi‘i Buyids controlled Baghdad, and from 1055 the Seljuk sultans: the caliph kept religious prestige but lost real power.","<b>Common confusion:</b> the “golden age” did not end when the caliphs lost power. Much of the great science and literature came from regional courts (Samanid Bukhara, Buyid Iran, Fatimid Cairo) that nominally deferred to or rivalled Baghdad."],"connections":["umayyad","wisdom","mamluk","caliph","falsafa"],"example":"Al-Mansur’s Round City of Baghdad (762), with the caliph’s palace and mosque at the centre of concentric walls.","numbers":[["Abbasid caliphate","750–1258"],["Baghdad founded","762"],["Buyids take Baghdad","945"],["Seljuks enter Baghdad","1055"],["Figurehead caliphs in Cairo","1261–1517"]],"numbersSource":"Encyclopaedia Britannica, “Abbasid caliphate” and “Baghdad”.","played":["750: the Umayyads are defeated at the Zab; the Abbasids take power.","762: al-Mansur founds Baghdad on the Tigris.","945: the Buyids hold Baghdad; the caliph becomes a figurehead.","1258: the Mongols sack Baghdad and kill the caliph al-Musta‘sim."],"graph":{"key":"ihum242Timeline","args":{"hi":"Abbasid"}},"starred":false},{"id":"mamluk","n":18,"week":3,"term":"Mamluks","simple":"Mamluks were slave-soldiers who became rulers, and their sultanate in Egypt and Syria (1250–1517) stopped the Mongols at ‘Ayn Jalut in 1260 before falling to the Ottomans.","formula":"<i>mamluk</i> · “owned”","more":["From the 9th century rulers bought boys from the steppe, mostly Turks, converted and trained them as an elite cavalry loyal to their master. In Egypt the mamluks of the Ayyubid sultan seized power in 1250 and ruled as a self-renewing military caste: new rulers usually came from newly bought recruits, not sons.","The sultanate defeated the Mongols, expelled the last Crusaders (Acre, 1291), hosted figurehead Abbasid caliphs in Cairo and filled Cairo with mosques, madrasas and tombs. It fell to the Ottomans in 1516–1517.","<b>Common confusion:</b> Mamluks vs Janissaries. Both were slave-origin soldiers, but Mamluks were bought from outside the realm and became rulers; Janissaries were levied from Ottoman Christian subjects and served the sultan."],"connections":["abbasid","devshirme","gunpowder","madrasa","caliph"],"example":"At ‘Ayn Jalut (1260) in Palestine, the Mamluk army under Qutuz and Baybars defeated a Mongol force, halting the Mongol advance on Egypt.","numbers":[["Mamluk sultanate","1250–1517"],["‘Ayn Jalut","3 September 1260"],["Fall of Acre, last Crusader stronghold","1291"]],"numbersSource":"Encyclopaedia Britannica, “Mamluk”, “Battle of ʿAyn Jālūt” and “Acre”.","played":["1250: mamluk officers seize power from the Ayyubids in Egypt.","1260: victory over the Mongols at ‘Ayn Jalut.","1261: a surviving Abbasid is installed as caliph in Cairo, lending legitimacy.","1516–1517: Ottoman Sultan Selim I conquers Syria and Egypt."],"graph":{"key":"ihum242Timeline","args":{"hi":"Mamluk"}},"starred":false},{"id":"gunpowder","n":19,"week":3,"term":"Gunpowder empires","simple":"The Ottoman (c. 1299–1922), Safavid (1501–1736) and Mughal (1526–1857) empires: three large early modern Muslim states built on firearms, standing armies and centralized administration.","more":["The label comes from the historians Marshall Hodgson and William McNeill: three large, long-lived states that used firearms and artillery, centralized bureaucracies and court-sponsored art. Many historians now treat it as a convenient grouping rather than an explanation.","They differed sharply. The Ottomans were Sunni (Hanafi) and ruled a multi-religious empire from the Balkans to Arabia. The Safavids grew out of a Sufi order and imposed Twelver Shi‘ism on Iran. The Mughals, descended from Timur and Chinggis Khan, ruled a mostly Hindu population; Akbar (r. 1556–1605) promoted tolerance.","<b>Common confusion:</b> “Ottoman” is not “Turkish” in the modern national sense, and Safavid Iran was not Shi‘i before 1501; most Iranians were then Sunni."],"connections":["devshirme","millet","sunnishia","mamluk","sufism"],"example":"At Chaldiran (1514) the Ottoman army, with artillery and Janissary musketeers, defeated the Safavid cavalry, fixing a frontier between Sunni Ottoman and Shi‘i Iranian lands.","numbers":[["Ottoman","c. 1299–1922"],["Safavid","1501–1736"],["Mughal","1526–1857"],["Constantinople taken","1453"]],"numbersSource":"Encyclopaedia Britannica, “Ottoman Empire”, “Safavid dynasty”, “Mughal dynasty”.","played":["1453: Mehmed II takes Constantinople, which becomes Istanbul.","1501: Shah Isma‘il I founds the Safavid state at Tabriz.","1514: the Ottomans defeat the Safavids at Chaldiran; 1526: Babur wins at Panipat and founds the Mughal empire.","1598: Shah ‘Abbas I makes Isfahan the Safavid capital."],"graph":{"key":"ihum242Timeline","args":{"hi":["Ottoman","Safavid","Mughal"]}},"starred":false},{"id":"devshirme","n":20,"week":3,"term":"Devshirme & Janissaries","simple":"The Ottoman levy of Christian boys, converted and trained for the Janissary infantry or palace and state service.","formula":"<i>devşirme</i> · “collection”","more":["Officials periodically took boys from Christian peasant families, mostly in the Balkans. The most promising were educated in the palace and could rise to the highest offices; the rest became <b>Janissaries</b>, the sultan’s salaried standing infantry, an early user of firearms.","Because they owed everything to the sultan, these “slaves of the Porte” were a counterweight to the landed cavalry and old families. The levy sat uneasily with the usual legal protection of dhimmis, and it faded in the 17th century as Janissary posts became hereditary and open to Muslims.","<b>Common confusion:</b> the devshirme is not the millet system and not the Mamluk institution. By the 18th century the Janissaries were a powerful, often rebellious corps; Mahmud II destroyed it in 1826."],"connections":["gunpowder","mamluk","millet","dhimmi"],"example":"The architect Sinan (c. 1490–1588) entered Ottoman service through the devshirme, served as a Janissary engineer, and became chief architect, designing the Süleymaniye Mosque.","numbers":[["Sokollu Mehmed Pasha, devshirme recruit, grand vizier","1565–1579"],["Janissary corps abolished","1826"]],"numbersSource":"Encyclopaedia Britannica, “devşirme”, “Janissary” and “Sokollu Mehmed Paşa”.","played":["A boy is taken from a Balkan village and sent to Anatolia to learn Turkish and Islam.","He is selected for the palace school or the Janissary corps.","Talent and loyalty bring promotion: governor, general, grand vizier.","1826: the Janissaries resist reform and are abolished by Mahmud II."],"starred":false},{"id":"millet","n":21,"week":3,"term":"Millet system","simple":"Ottoman arrangement in which religious communities (e.g. Greek Orthodox, Armenian, Jewish) ran their own personal-status law and internal affairs under their leaders.","formula":"<i>millet</i> · religious community, nation","more":["Each community’s religious head was responsible to the state for its taxes and order. Within it, its own courts handled marriage, divorce and inheritance, and it ran its own churches, synagogues, schools and charities.","Historians such as Benjamin Braude argue that a formal, empire-wide “millet system” is largely a 19th-century creation; earlier arrangements were local and ad hoc, built on the older dhimmi framework. In the Tanzimat era communities received written constitutions, and “millet” shifted toward meaning “nation”.","<b>Common confusion:</b> the millet arrangement did not mean equality: non-Muslims kept dhimmi status until the reforms of 1839–1856. Its legacy survives in religious personal-status courts in Lebanon and Israel."],"connections":["dhimmi","tanzimat","gunpowder","devshirme"],"example":"After taking Constantinople in 1453, Mehmed II installed Gennadios Scholarios as Greek Orthodox Patriarch, responsible for the Orthodox community.","played":["1453: Mehmed II appoints a patriarch to lead the Orthodox.","Community courts settle a Jewish or Armenian family’s inheritance by their own law.","1856: the Reform Edict promises equality to all subjects.","1863: the Armenian National Constitution reorganizes that community with an elected assembly."],"graph":"ihum242Millet","starred":false},{"id":"qasida","n":22,"week":4,"term":"Qasida","simple":"The classical Arabic ode: a long poem in one rhyme and meter that traditionally opens with a nostalgic prelude (nasib) at an abandoned campsite, then moves through a journey to a main theme such as praise.","more":["Pre-Islamic poets perfected the form; the most famous odes were later gathered as the <i>Mu‘allaqat</i> (“suspended” poems). The critic Ibn Qutayba (d. 889) described the three-part scheme: <b>nasib</b> (lost love at the ruined camp), <b>rahil</b> (the desert journey) and the main purpose: praise (<i>madih</i>), boasting or satire.","Under the caliphs it became the court poem of praise. Poets in Persian, Turkish and Urdu adopted it, and religious qasidas praise the Prophet.","<b>Common confusion:</b> qasida vs ghazal. The qasida is long, public and often panegyric; the ghazal is a shorter love lyric that grew out of the qasida’s amatory opening."],"connections":["ghazal","adab","quran"],"example":"Imru’ al-Qays’s <i>Mu‘allaqa</i> opens “Stop, let us weep at the memory of a beloved and an abode”, the classic nasib at the deserted campsite.","played":["Nasib: the poet halts at the traces of a deserted camp and remembers his beloved.","Rahil: he rides off across the desert, praising his camel’s endurance.","Madih: he arrives at the patron and praises his generosity and courage.","At Sayf al-Dawla’s court in Aleppo, al-Mutanabbi (915–965) made this form famous."],"graph":"ihum242Qasida","starred":false},{"id":"ghazal","n":23,"week":4,"term":"Ghazal","simple":"A short love lyric in monorhyme couplets, central to Persian poetry (Hafez), in which earthly and divine love often blur.","more":["Arab poets of the Umayyad period made love poetry an independent form; Persian poets turned the ghazal into a tightly built lyric of about 5–15 couplets. The first couplet rhymes in both lines; afterwards only the second line of each couplet does, often followed by a repeated refrain (<i>radif</i>). The poet names himself in the last couplet (<i>takhallus</i>).","Its stock images, wine, the tavern, the cruel beloved, the nightingale and the rose, can be read as worldly or as Sufi symbols of divine love and intoxication with God. The ambiguity is part of the art.","<b>Common confusion:</b> ghazal vs <i>ruba‘i</i> (a single quatrain, as in Omar Khayyam) vs <i>masnavi</i> (long narrative poetry in rhyming couplets, as in Rumi’s <i>Masnavi</i>)."],"connections":["qasida","sufism","adab"],"example":"The <i>Divan</i> of Hafez (c. 1325–1390) of Shiraz: Iranians still open it at random to read their fortune (<i>fal-e Hafez</i>).","played":["Couplet 1 sets the rhyme and refrain in both lines.","Each later couplet is a self-contained image, linked by rhyme and mood rather than story.","Wine and the beloved can be read literally or mystically.","The last couplet names the poet, often addressing himself."],"graph":"ihum242Ghazal","starred":false},{"id":"adab","n":24,"week":4,"term":"Adab","simple":"Refined culture and etiquette, and the literature of the cultivated person: anthologies, belles-lettres, wit and moral instruction.","formula":"<i>adab</i> · manners, refinement; (later) literature","more":["Adab was the knowledge expected of a cultured courtier or secretary (<i>katib</i>): poetry, history, proverbs, anecdotes and good manners. It drew on Arabic, Persian and Greek sources and was meant to delight and instruct.","Masters include al-Jahiz of Basra (d. 868/869), essayist on everything from animals to misers, and Ibn al-Muqaffa‘ (8th c.), who translated <i>Kalila wa-Dimna</i> from Middle Persian. In modern Arabic, <i>adab</i> simply means “literature”.","<b>Common confusion:</b> adab is not religious law or ritual etiquette alone; it is a secular-leaning humanistic culture that coexisted with religious learning."],"connections":["qasida","ghazal","wisdom","nahda"],"example":"<i>Kalila wa-Dimna</i>, animal fables of Indian origin about the conduct of kings, passed through Middle Persian into Arabic and then into many languages.","played":["A young secretary memorizes poetry, proverbs and model letters.","He reads anthologies like Ibn Qutayba’s <i>‘Uyun al-akhbar</i> (“Choice Reports”).","Later he enjoys al-Hariri’s <i>Maqamat</i> (early 12th c.), virtuoso tales of a silver-tongued rogue.","A 1237 manuscript of the <i>Maqamat</i> illustrated by al-Wasiti shows how adab and painting met."],"starred":false},{"id":"muqarnas","n":25,"week":4,"term":"Muqarnas","simple":"Honeycomb or “stalactite” vaulting built from tiers of small niches, used in domes, portals and transitions, as in the Alhambra.","more":["Muqarnas appears from about the 10th–11th centuries, in Iran and Iraq and in North Africa. Each tier of small niches projects over the one below, so it can turn a square corner into the round base of a dome, or fill a portal’s half-dome.","It is often non-structural: carved in stone, or hung in plaster or wood beneath the real roof. Built from a small set of repeated units, it spread across the Islamic world and beyond, to the 1140s ceiling of the Cappella Palatina in Norman Palermo.","<b>Common confusion:</b> muqarnas is three-dimensional vaulting; arabesque is flat ornament. A mihrab may contain muqarnas, but it is a different thing."],"connections":["arabesque","mosqueparts"],"example":"The ceiling of the Hall of the Abencerrajes in the Alhambra, Granada (14th c., Nasrid), an eight-pointed star of thousands of muqarnas cells.","played":["A square hall needs a round dome.","At the corners, a first row of small niches steps out.","Each higher tier projects further, closing the gap.","The top tier forms the circle on which the dome rests."],"graph":"ihum242Muqarnas","starred":false},{"id":"arabesque","n":26,"week":4,"term":"Arabesque","simple":"Infinitely extendable patterns of scrolling, interlacing vegetal forms, alongside geometric patterns and calligraphy in Islamic ornament.","more":["“Arabesque” is a European term; in Persian and Turkish the vegetal scroll is called <i>islimi</i>. Stems split and rejoin so that the pattern has no natural end, suited to covering whole surfaces of stucco, tile, wood and manuscripts.","Islamic ornament combines three systems: <b>vegetal</b> (arabesque), <b>geometric</b> (star and polygon patterns) and <b>calligraphic</b> (Qur’anic and poetic inscriptions). Religious buildings generally avoid images of people and animals.","<b>Common confusion:</b> “Islam forbids all images” is too simple. Figurative painting flourished in palaces and illustrated books, such as Persian and Mughal miniatures; the avoidance applies mainly to religious spaces."],"connections":["muqarnas","mosqueparts","umayyad"],"example":"The mosaics of the Dome of the Rock (691–692) show scrolling vines, trees and jewelled vessels with no human or animal figures.","played":["Start from a single stem.","It splits into a leaf and a new stem, which curls back.","The new stem splits again, interlacing with the first.","Repeated across a wall, the pattern runs on without end."],"starred":false},{"id":"mosqueparts","n":27,"week":4,"term":"Mihrab, minbar, minaret, iwan","simple":"The key parts of a mosque: the mihrab (niche marking the qibla, the direction of Mecca), the minbar (pulpit for the Friday sermon), the minaret (tower for the call to prayer) and, in Iranian plans, the iwan (vaulted hall open on one side).","more":["Prayer is performed in rows facing the <b>qibla</b>, so congregational mosques are often broad rather than deep. The first mosque was the courtyard of the Prophet’s house in Medina; the concave mihrab became standard from the Umayyad period.","Regional plans differ: the Arab <b>hypostyle</b> hall with a courtyard (Córdoba), the Iranian <b>four-iwan</b> courtyard plan (Isfahan) and the Ottoman <b>central-dome</b> plan (Sinan’s Istanbul mosques).","<b>Common confusion:</b> the mihrab is not an altar and nothing is offered there; it only marks direction. The imam leads prayer near it, but preaches from the minbar."],"connections":["muqarnas","madrasa","umayyad","arabesque","hijra"],"example":"In the Great Mosque of Córdoba, begun c. 785, a forest of double arches leads to the 10th-century mihrab of al-Hakam II, decorated with Byzantine-style gold mosaics.","numbers":[["Great Mosque of Córdoba begun","c. 785"],["Süleymaniye Mosque, Istanbul","1550–1557"],["Süleymaniye dome height","≈ 53 m"],["Süleymaniye dome diameter","≈ 27 m"]],"numbersSource":"Encyclopaedia Britannica, “Mosque-Cathedral of Córdoba” and “Süleymaniye Mosque” (sources give the dome as 26–27 m across).","played":["Enter the courtyard and wash at the ablution fountain.","Inside, stand in rows parallel to the qibla wall.","The imam leads the prayer from in front of the mihrab.","On Friday he preaches the sermon (<i>khutba</i>) from the minbar."],"graph":"ihum242MosquePlan","starred":false},{"id":"tanzimat","n":28,"week":5,"term":"Tanzimat (1839–1876)","simple":"Ottoman reform era: centralization, legal equality of subjects regardless of religion, new courts, schools and codes on European models.","formula":"<i>tanzimat</i> · reorganizations","more":["Statesmen such as Mustafa Reşid Pasha sought to save the empire from European pressure and internal revolt by strengthening the central state. The <b>Gülhane edict</b> of 1839 promised security of life and property and fair taxation; the <b>Reform Edict</b> of 1856 declared Muslims and non-Muslims equal.","New secular courts, schools and codes followed. The era closed with the first Ottoman constitution (1876), which Abdülhamid II suspended in 1878.","<b>Common confusion:</b> the Tanzimat is not the Young Turk revolution (1908), and not simple Westernization: the <i>Mecelle</i> civil code (1869–1876) codified Hanafi law in modern form."],"connections":["millet","modernism","dhimmi","waqf","nahda"],"example":"The <i>Mecelle</i>, drafted by a commission under Ahmed Cevdet Pasha, codified Islamic civil law in numbered articles like a European code; parts stayed in force in some successor states for decades.","numbers":[["Gülhane edict","3 November 1839"],["Reform Edict (equality)","1856"],["First Ottoman constitution","1876"],["Constitution suspended","1878"]],"numbersSource":"Encyclopaedia Britannica, “Tanzimat” and “Ottoman Empire”.","played":["1826: the Janissaries are abolished, clearing the way for a new army.","1839: the Gülhane edict opens the Tanzimat.","1856: the Reform Edict declares legal equality and ends the jizya.","1876: a constitution and parliament; suspended two years later."],"graph":{"key":"ihum242Modern","args":{"hi":[1839,1876]}},"starred":false},{"id":"nahda","n":29,"week":5,"term":"Nahda","simple":"The Arab “awakening/renaissance” of the 19th and early 20th centuries: printing, journalism, translation, new literary forms and the revival of classical Arabic.","formula":"<i>nahda</i> · rising, awakening","more":["Centred on Cairo and Beirut, it was carried by Muslim and Christian intellectuals alike: the government press at Bulaq, Muhammad ‘Ali’s educational missions to Europe, Butrus al-Bustani’s encyclopaedia and dictionary, and dozens of journals.","Writers revived classical Arabic style while adopting new forms (the novel, drama, the newspaper essay), and debated how to be modern without simply copying Europe. It fed both Islamic modernism and Arab nationalism.","<b>Common confusion:</b> the Nahda is cultural and literary; the Tanzimat is Ottoman state reform. Historians also now question the old story of a long “decline” before the Nahda."],"connections":["adab","modernism","orientalism","tanzimat","sykes"],"example":"Rifa‘a al-Tahtawi (1801–1873), imam to an Egyptian student mission in Paris (1826–1831), described French society and institutions in <i>Takhlis al-ibriz</i> (1834).","played":["1798: Napoleon’s invasion of Egypt shocks Ottoman Arab elites.","1820s: Muhammad ‘Ali founds the Bulaq press and sends students to Europe.","Later 19th century: journals and translations multiply in Beirut and Cairo.","1988: Naguib Mahfouz (1911–2006), heir to the modern Arabic novel, wins the Nobel Prize."],"starred":false},{"id":"modernism","n":30,"week":5,"term":"Islamic modernism","simple":"Late 19th-century reform movement (al-Afghani, Muhammad ‘Abduh) arguing that Islam is compatible with reason, science and modern institutions.","more":["Modernists argued that Muslims had fallen behind not because of Islam but because of blind imitation (<i>taqlid</i>) of past jurists. They called for renewed <i>ijtihad</i>, modern education and a return to the faith’s original sources as they read them.","Jamal al-Din al-Afghani (1838/39–1897) preached pan-Islamic unity against European imperialism. His disciple Muhammad ‘Abduh (1849–1905), Grand Mufti of Egypt from 1899, pursued legal and educational reform; Rashid Rida carried the project on in the journal <i>al-Manar</i>. In India, Sayyid Ahmad Khan founded the Aligarh college (1875).","<b>Common confusion:</b> modernism is neither secularism nor later Islamism, though both drew on it. Rida’s later thought moved toward more conservative Salafi positions."],"connections":["nahda","tanzimat","ulama","sharia","orientalism"],"example":"‘Abduh’s <i>Theology of Unity</i> (<i>Risalat al-tawhid</i>) argued that reason and revelation cannot truly conflict.","played":["Problem: European military, economic and colonial dominance.","Diagnosis: not Islam, but taqlid and neglect of reason.","Remedy: ijtihad, science and reformed education, judged by the Qur’an and sunna.","Result: reforms at al-Azhar and in law; later movements claim the legacy in different directions."],"starred":false},{"id":"sykes","n":31,"week":5,"term":"Sykes–Picot (1916)","simple":"A secret Anglo-French agreement of 1916 dividing the Ottoman Arab lands into spheres of influence, which remains a symbol of colonial borders in the Middle East.","more":["Negotiated by Mark Sykes and François Georges-Picot in 1916, it gave France a sphere in Syria and Lebanon and Britain one in Iraq and toward the Gulf, with Palestine to be international. It conflicted with British promises to Sharif Husayn of Mecca (the Husayn–McMahon correspondence) and with the Balfour Declaration (1917).","After the war the San Remo conference (1920) assigned League of Nations mandates: Syria and Lebanon to France; Iraq, Palestine and Transjordan to Britain.","<b>Common confusion:</b> Sykes–Picot did not itself draw today’s borders; those came from later postwar settlements. It endures as a symbol of Western betrayal because it was secret and revealed by the Bolsheviks in 1917."],"connections":["orientalism","nahda"],"example":"The French Mandate for Syria and Lebanon (from 1920), with the separate state of Greater Lebanon proclaimed in 1920.","numbers":[["Agreement concluded","May 1916"],["Published by the Bolsheviks","November 1917"],["San Remo conference","April 1920"]],"numbersSource":"Encyclopaedia Britannica, “Sykes-Picot Agreement” and “San Remo Conference”.","played":["1915–1916: Britain courts Sharif Husayn with promises of Arab independence.","1916: Britain and France secretly divide the region in the Sykes–Picot agreement.","1917: the Bolsheviks publish the secret treaty.","1920: San Remo assigns the mandates; 1924: the caliphate is abolished."],"graph":{"key":"ihum242Modern","args":{"hi":1916}},"starred":false},{"id":"orientalism","n":32,"week":5,"term":"Orientalism (Said, 1978)","simple":"Edward Said’s argument that Western scholarship, art and literature constructed “the Orient” as an exotic, inferior Other, a body of knowledge entangled with imperial power.","more":["Drawing on Michel Foucault’s idea of discourse, Edward Said (1935–2003) argued that European writers, travellers, painters and scholars produced a stable image of an unchanging, irrational, sensual East that justified rule over it. Knowledge and power reinforced each other.","The book helped found postcolonial studies. Critics such as Bernard Lewis and Robert Irwin defended the scholarly tradition; Sadik al-Azm warned of an “Orientalism in reverse” that essentializes the West.","<b>Common confusion:</b> “Orientalism” has three senses: the academic study of Asian languages and cultures, a 19th-century style of painting, and Said’s critical sense of a discourse of power."],"connections":["sykes","nahda","modernism"],"example":"Jean-Léon Gérôme’s painting <i>The Snake Charmer</i> (c. 1879), a timeless, exotic East, appears on the cover of a widely sold edition of <i>Orientalism</i>.","played":["A European traveller writes that “the Oriental” is timeless and irrational.","Scholars and novelists repeat the image; it becomes common knowledge.","Administrators cite that knowledge to justify colonial rule.","Said (1978) shows the circle, and asks who has the power to represent whom."],"graph":{"key":"ihum242Modern","args":{"hi":1978}},"starred":false}]};
const GRAPH_SRC = "// ECON 381 card graphs.\n//\n// Each entry returns { svg, cap } for one card's \"Graph\" section. Drawn as\n// inline SVG from code rather than shipped as images, so they take the\n// card's week colour (via CSS classes in the page), stay sharp at any size\n// and keep the whole deck in one pasteable file.\n//\n// Plain ES5-ish code on purpose: this file is pasted into the review page's\n// <script> as-is, so it must not use backticks or \"${\".\n(function () {\n  var W = 320, H = 210;\n  var X0 = 42, Y0 = 178, X1 = 304, Y1 = 16;   // plot box: origin bottom-left\n\n  function svg(inner, h) {\n    return '<svg viewBox=\"0 0 ' + W + ' ' + (h || H) + '\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">' +\n      '<defs><marker id=\"ah\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"8\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\">' +\n      '<path d=\"M0 0L10 5L0 10z\" fill=\"#2B2118\" opacity=\".7\"/></marker></defs>' + inner + '</svg>';\n  }\n  function t(x, y, s, cls, anchor) {\n    return '<text x=\"' + r(x) + '\" y=\"' + r(y) + '\"' + (cls ? ' class=\"' + cls + '\"' : '') +\n      (anchor ? ' text-anchor=\"' + anchor + '\"' : '') + '>' + s + '</text>';\n  }\n  function ln(x1, y1, x2, y2, cls, arrow) {\n    return '<line x1=\"' + r(x1) + '\" y1=\"' + r(y1) + '\" x2=\"' + r(x2) + '\" y2=\"' + r(y2) + '\" class=\"' + (cls || 'c4') + '\"' +\n      (arrow ? ' marker-end=\"url(#ah)\"' : '') + '/>';\n  }\n  function dot(x, y, cls) { return '<circle cx=\"' + r(x) + '\" cy=\"' + r(y) + '\" r=\"3.6\" class=\"' + (cls || 'dot') + '\"/>'; }\n  function rect(x, y, w, h, cls, rx) {\n    return '<rect x=\"' + r(x) + '\" y=\"' + r(y) + '\" width=\"' + r(w) + '\" height=\"' + r(h) + '\" rx=\"' + (rx == null ? 3 : rx) + '\" class=\"' + cls + '\"/>';\n  }\n  function r(v) { return Math.round(v * 10) / 10; }\n  function pathD(pts) {\n    return pts.map(function (p, k) { return (k ? 'L' : 'M') + r(p[0]) + ' ' + r(p[1]); }).join('');\n  }\n  function curve(pts, cls, arrow) {\n    return '<path d=\"' + pathD(pts) + '\" class=\"' + cls + '\"' + (arrow ? ' marker-end=\"url(#ah)\"' : '') + '/>';\n  }\n  function area(pts, cls) { return '<path d=\"' + pathD(pts) + 'Z\" class=\"' + cls + '\"/>'; }\n  /** Samples f over [a,b] in data units and maps it into the plot box. */\n  function plot(f, a, b, sx, sy, n) {\n    var pts = [];\n    n = n || 60;\n    for (var k = 0; k <= n; k++) {\n      var x = a + (b - a) * k / n;\n      pts.push([sx(x), sy(f(x))]);\n    }\n    return pts;\n  }\n  function scale(d0, d1, p0, p1) { return function (v) { return p0 + (v - d0) * (p1 - p0) / (d1 - d0); }; }\n  function axes(xl, yl) {\n    return ln(X0, Y1 - 4, X0, Y0, 'ax') + ln(X0, Y0, X1 + 4, Y0, 'ax') +\n      t(X1 + 2, Y0 + 15, xl, 'it', 'end') + t(X0 + 6, Y1 + 2, yl, 'it', 'start');\n  }\n  function vguide(x, y, label, cls) {\n    return ln(x, y, x, Y0, 'c4 dash faint') + (label ? t(x, Y0 + 14, label, cls || 'it', 'middle') : '');\n  }\n  function hguide(x, y, label, cls) {\n    return ln(X0, y, x, y, 'c4 dash faint') + (label ? t(X0 - 5, y + 4, label, cls || 'it', 'end') : '');\n  }\n\n  var G = {};\n\n  // ------------------------------------------------------------ week 1\n\n  G.businessCycle = function () {\n    var sx = scale(0, 10, X0, X1), sy = scale(0, 10, Y0, Y1);\n    var trend = function (x) { return 2 + 0.62 * x; };\n    var act = function (x) { return trend(x) + 1.1 * Math.sin(x * 1.25) - (x > 5.6 && x < 7.6 ? 0.9 * Math.sin((x - 5.6) * Math.PI / 2) : 0); };\n    var o = axes('time', 'real GDP');\n    o += rect(sx(5.55), Y1, sx(7.1) - sx(5.55), Y0 - Y1, 'fill2', 0);\n    o += t((sx(5.55) + sx(7.1)) / 2, Y1 + 12, 'recession', 'k2', 'middle');\n    o += curve(plot(trend, 0, 10, sx, sy), 'c4 dash');\n    o += curve(plot(act, 0, 10, sx, sy, 120), 'c1');\n    o += t(sx(9.9), sy(trend(9.9)) + 16, 'trend', 'lb', 'end');\n    o += t(sx(1.9), sy(act(1.3)) - 8, 'actual', 'k1', 'middle');\n    return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'Output falls below its trend; the shaded stretch is the recession. Rule of thumb: two quarters of falling real GDP.' };\n  };\n\n  G.circularFlow = function () {\n    var o = '';\n    o += rect(18, 78, 92, 50, 'box1', 12) + t(64, 108, 'Households', 'b', 'middle');\n    o += rect(210, 78, 92, 50, 'box2', 12) + t(256, 108, 'Firms', 'b', 'middle');\n    o += curve([[64, 74], [64, 30], [256, 30], [256, 72]], 'c1', true);\n    o += t(160, 24, 'Spending ($) = expenditure', 'k1', 'middle');\n    o += curve([[256, 132], [256, 178], [64, 178], [64, 134]], 'c2', true);\n    o += t(160, 194, 'Wages, rent, profit ($) = income', 'k2', 'middle');\n    o += curve([[240, 72], [240, 48], [80, 48], [80, 72]], 'c4 dash', true);\n    o += t(160, 62, 'goods & services', 'lb', 'middle');\n    o += curve([[80, 134], [80, 158], [240, 158], [240, 134]], 'c4 dash', true);\n    o += t(160, 152, 'labor & capital', 'lb', 'middle');\n    return { svg: svg(o, 205), cap: 'The same dollars measured three ways: what is spent, what is earned, what is produced. All three equal GDP.' };\n  };\n\n  G.gdpShares = function (a) {\n    var rows = [['C', 'Consumption', 68], ['I', 'Investment', 18], ['G', 'Government', 17], ['NX', 'Net exports', -3]];\n    var sx = scale(-10, 75, 92, 300);\n    var o = ln(sx(0), 14, sx(0), 186, 'c4');\n    rows.forEach(function (row, k) {\n      var y = 22 + k * 42;\n      var on = !a.hi || a.hi === row[0];\n      var x0 = Math.min(sx(0), sx(row[2])), w = Math.abs(sx(row[2]) - sx(0));\n      o += rect(x0, y, w, 26, on ? 'solid1' : 'solid4', 5);\n      o += t(84, y + 12, row[0], 'b', 'end') + t(84, y + 24, row[1], 'lb', 'end');\n      // A negative bar's label goes on the positive side of the zero line, so\n      // it never runs into the row's name.\n      o += t(row[2] < 0 ? sx(0) + 5 : x0 + w + 4, y + 17, (row[2] > 0 ? '' : '−') + Math.abs(row[2]) + '%', on ? 'k1' : 'lb', 'start');\n    });\n    return { svg: svg(o, 190), cap: 'Approximate shares of US GDP, 2025 (BEA). Net exports are negative: the US imports more than it exports.' };\n  };\n\n  G.stockFlow = function () {\n    var o = '';\n    o += curve([[96, 40], [96, 22], [150, 22]], 'c4');\n    o += rect(140, 16, 22, 12, 'box', 3);\n    o += curve([[151, 28], [151, 70]], 'c1', true);\n    o += t(160, 52, 'inflow per hour = FLOW', 'k1');\n    o += '<path d=\"M70 70 L84 176 Q86 184 96 184 L224 184 Q234 184 236 176 L250 70\" class=\"c4\"/>';\n    o += area([[77, 118], [84, 176], [90, 182], [230, 182], [236, 176], [243, 118]], 'fill1');\n    o += ln(77, 118, 243, 118, 'c1');\n    o += t(160, 156, 'water in the tub = STOCK', 'k1', 'middle');\n    o += t(160, 170, '(measured at a moment)', 'lb', 'middle');\n    o += curve([[236, 176], [266, 196]], 'c2', true);\n    o += t(268, 192, 'outflow', 'k2');\n    return { svg: svg(o, 205), cap: 'Investment (a flow) fills the capital stock; depreciation drains it. GDP is a flow, wealth is a stock.' };\n  };\n\n  G.valueAdded = function () {\n    var steps = [['Farmer', 'wheat', 1, 1], ['Miller', 'flour', 3, 2], ['Baker', 'bread', 5, 2]];\n    var o = '';\n    var sy = scale(0, 5, 170, 30);\n    steps.forEach(function (s, k) {\n      var x = 30 + k * 98;\n      o += rect(x, sy(s[2]), 70, sy(0) - sy(s[2]), 'solid4', 5);\n      o += rect(x, sy(s[2]), 70, sy(0) - sy(s[3]), 'solid1', 5);\n      o += t(x + 35, sy(s[2]) - 6, '$' + s[2] + ' ' + s[1], 'b', 'middle');\n      o += t(x + 35, 186, s[0], 'lb', 'middle');\n      o += t(x + 35, sy(s[2]) + 16, '+' + s[3], 'w', 'middle');\n    });\n    o += t(300, 22, 'GDP = $5', 'k1', 'end');\n    return { svg: svg(o, 196), cap: 'Blue = value added at each stage ($1 + $2 + $2 = $5), which equals the final sale. Adding the full sales ($9) would double count.' };\n  };\n\n  G.indexBias = function () {\n    var sx = scale(0, 10, X0, X1), sy = scale(100, 132, Y0, Y1);\n    var o = axes('years from base', 'index');\n    o += curve(plot(function (x) { return 100 + 2.9 * x; }, 0, 10, sx, sy), 'c2');\n    o += curve(plot(function (x) { return 100 + 2.6 * x; }, 0, 10, sx, sy), 'c4 dash');\n    o += curve(plot(function (x) { return 100 + 2.3 * x; }, 0, 10, sx, sy), 'c1');\n    o += t(sx(10), sy(129) - 6, 'Laspeyres (CPI)', 'k2', 'end');\n    o += t(sx(10), sy(126) - 6, 'true cost of living', 'lb', 'end');\n    o += t(sx(10), sy(123) + 14, 'Paasche (deflator)', 'k1', 'end');\n    o += t(X0 - 6, sy(100) + 4, '100', 'lb', 'end');\n    return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'Stylised. The fixed basket ignores switching to cheaper goods, so it runs high; the current basket gives too little weight to what got dearer, so it runs low.' };\n  };\n\n  G.cpiHistory = function () {\n    var d = [[2019, 1.8], [2020, 1.2], [2021, 4.7], [2022, 8.0], [2023, 4.1], [2024, 2.9]];\n    var sx = scale(2018.4, 2024.6, X0, X1), sy = scale(0, 9, Y0, Y1);\n    var o = axes('', '%');\n    [2, 4, 6, 8].forEach(function (v) { o += ln(X0, sy(v), X1, sy(v), 'grid') + t(X0 - 5, sy(v) + 4, v, 'lb', 'end'); });\n    d.forEach(function (p) {\n      o += rect(sx(p[0]) - 15, sy(p[1]), 30, Y0 - sy(p[1]), p[0] === 2022 ? 'solid2' : 'solid1', 4);\n      o += t(sx(p[0]), sy(p[1]) - 5, p[1].toFixed(1), p[0] === 2022 ? 'k2' : 'lb', 'middle');\n      o += t(sx(p[0]), Y0 + 14, String(p[0]), 'lb', 'middle');\n    });\n    return { svg: svg(o, 200), cap: 'US CPI-U inflation, annual average (BLS). 2022 was the highest since 1981.' };\n  };\n\n  G.laborForceTree = function () {\n    var o = '';\n    o += rect(95, 8, 130, 34, 'box', 10) + t(160, 24, 'Adult population', 'b', 'middle') + t(160, 36, '16+, not institutionalised', 'lb', 'middle');\n    o += curve([[160, 42], [160, 54], [82, 54], [82, 66]], 'c4', true);\n    o += curve([[160, 54], [240, 54], [240, 66]], 'c4', true);\n    o += rect(14, 68, 136, 30, 'box1', 10) + t(82, 87, 'Labor force  L', 'k1', 'middle');\n    o += rect(170, 68, 140, 30, 'box', 10) + t(240, 87, 'Not in labor force', 'b', 'middle');\n    o += curve([[50, 98], [50, 112]], 'c4', true) + curve([[114, 98], [114, 112]], 'c4', true);\n    o += rect(14, 114, 70, 46, 'box3', 10) + t(49, 133, 'Employed', 'k3', 'middle') + t(49, 148, 'E', 'it', 'middle');\n    o += rect(88, 114, 62, 46, 'box2', 10) + t(119, 133, 'Unem-', 'k2', 'middle') + t(119, 146, 'ployed  U', 'k2', 'middle');\n    o += rect(170, 114, 140, 46, 'box', 10) + t(240, 131, 'retirees, students,', 'lb', 'middle') + t(240, 145, 'homemakers,', 'lb', 'middle') + t(240, 156, 'discouraged workers', 'k2', 'middle');\n    o += t(82, 182, 'u = U ⁄ (E + U)', 'it', 'middle');\n    o += t(240, 182, 'LFPR = L ⁄ adult pop.', 'it', 'middle');\n    return { svg: svg(o, 192), cap: 'A discouraged worker who stops searching moves from U to \"not in the labor force\": the unemployment rate falls though nothing improved.' };\n  };\n\n  // ------------------------------------------------------------ week 2\n\n  G.production = function (a) {\n    var v = a.x || 'L';\n    var sx = scale(0, 10, X0, X1), sy = scale(0, 10, Y0, Y1);\n    var f = function (x) { return 3 * Math.pow(x, 0.5); };\n    var o = axes(v, 'Y');\n    o += curve(plot(f, 0, 10, sx, sy, 80), 'c1');\n    [2.2, 7].forEach(function (x0) {\n      var m = 1.5 / Math.sqrt(x0);\n      o += ln(sx(x0 - 1.6), sy(f(x0) - 1.6 * m), sx(x0 + 1.6), sy(f(x0) + 1.6 * m), 'c2');\n      o += dot(sx(x0), sy(f(x0)));\n    });\n    o += t(sx(2.2) + 4, sy(f(2.2)) + 18, 'steep: high MP' + v, 'k2');\n    o += t(sx(7) + 2, sy(f(7)) + 20, 'flatter: low MP' + v, 'k2');\n    o += t(sx(9.8), sy(f(9.8)) - 8, 'Y = F(K, L)', 'k1', 'end');\n    return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'Holding the other input fixed, each extra unit of ' + v + ' adds less output: diminishing marginal product. The slope is MP' + v + '.' };\n  };\n\n  G.factorMarket = function (a) {\n    var cap = a.kind === 'capital';\n    var v = cap ? 'K' : 'L', price = cap ? 'R/P' : 'W/P', mp = cap ? 'MPK' : 'MPL';\n    var sx = scale(0, 10, X0, X1), sy = scale(0, 10, Y0, Y1);\n    var d = function (x) { return 9.4 / (0.55 + 0.32 * x); };\n    var o = axes(v, price);\n    o += curve(plot(d, 1.4, 10, sx, sy), 'c1');\n    o += ln(sx(5.5), Y1, sx(5.5), Y0, 'c2');\n    o += t(sx(5.5) + 4, Y1 + 10, cap ? 'K̅ supply' : 'L̅ supply', 'k2');\n    o += hguide(sx(5.5), sy(d(5.5)), price + '*', 'k1');\n    o += dot(sx(5.5), sy(d(5.5)));\n    o += t(sx(9.9), sy(d(9.9)) - 8, mp + ' = demand', 'k1', 'end');\n    return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'Firms hire ' + v + ' until ' + mp + ' = ' + price + '. With the supply fixed, the ' + mp + ' at that quantity sets the real ' + (cap ? 'rental price' : 'wage') + '.' };\n  };\n\n  G.incomeSplit = function (a) {\n    var alpha = a.alpha || 1 / 3;\n    var cx = 108, cy = 98, R = 76, rr = 44;\n    function arc(a0, a1, cls) {\n      var p = function (ang, rad) { return [cx + rad * Math.sin(ang), cy - rad * Math.cos(ang)]; };\n      var A = p(a0, R), B = p(a1, R), C = p(a1, rr), D = p(a0, rr);\n      var big = a1 - a0 > Math.PI ? 1 : 0;\n      return '<path class=\"' + cls + '\" d=\"M' + r(A[0]) + ' ' + r(A[1]) + 'A' + R + ' ' + R + ' 0 ' + big + ' 1 ' + r(B[0]) + ' ' + r(B[1]) +\n        'L' + r(C[0]) + ' ' + r(C[1]) + 'A' + rr + ' ' + rr + ' 0 ' + big + ' 0 ' + r(D[0]) + ' ' + r(D[1]) + 'Z\"/>';\n    }\n    var cut = 2 * Math.PI * (1 - alpha);\n    var o = arc(0, cut, 'solid1') + arc(cut, 2 * Math.PI - 0.0001, 'solid2');\n    o += t(cx, cy + 4, 'Y', 'it b', 'middle');\n    o += rect(208, 64, 14, 14, 'solid1', 3) + t(228, 75, 'Labor  1 − α', 'k1');\n    o += t(228, 89, '≈ ' + Math.round((1 - alpha) * 100) + '% = WL/PY', 'lb');\n    o += rect(208, 108, 14, 14, 'solid2', 3) + t(228, 119, 'Capital  α', 'k2');\n    o += t(228, 133, '≈ ' + Math.round(alpha * 100) + '% = RK/PY', 'lb');\n    return { svg: svg(o, 186), cap: 'With Cobb-Douglas and competitive markets the pie is split in fixed shares, and the two slices use up all of output.' };\n  };\n\n  G.laborShareShift = function () {\n    var sx = scale(0, 10, X0, X1), sy = scale(50, 66, Y0, Y1);\n    var o = axes('time', 'labor share %');\n    o += curve(plot(function (x) { return 63.5 - 0.15 * Math.sin(x * 2.2) - (x > 5 ? 0.9 * (x - 5) : 0); }, 0, 10, sx, sy, 80), 'c1');\n    o += hguide(sx(5), sy(63.4), '', '');\n    o += t(sx(2.3), sy(64.6), 'roughly flat: Kaldor fact', 'lb', 'middle');\n    o += t(sx(9.6), sy(58.6) + 6, 'falls since ~2000', 'k1', 'end');\n    o += t(sx(9.6), sy(55) + 4, 'automation, offshoring,', 'lb', 'end');\n    o += t(sx(9.6), sy(55) + 16, 'rising markups', 'lb', 'end');\n    return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'Stylised. Cobb-Douglas says the share should be constant; the data show a decline, which is why the course asks you for explanations.' };\n  };\n\n  G.consumption = function () {\n    var sx = scale(0, 10, X0, X1), sy = scale(0, 10, Y0, Y1);\n    var o = axes('Y − T', 'C');\n    o += curve(plot(function (x) { return x; }, 0, 10, sx, sy), 'c4 dash faint');\n    o += curve(plot(function (x) { return 2 + 0.6 * x; }, 0, 10, sx, sy), 'c1');\n    o += ln(sx(4), sy(4.4), sx(6.5), sy(4.4), 'c2') + ln(sx(6.5), sy(4.4), sx(6.5), sy(5.9), 'c2');\n    o += t(sx(5.25), sy(4.4) + 14, '+$1', 'k2', 'middle') + t(sx(6.5) + 4, sy(5.15) + 4, '+$MPC', 'k2');\n    o += t(sx(9.8), sy(7.9) - 8, 'C = C(Y − T)', 'k1', 'end');\n    o += t(sx(9.8), sy(9.8) - 6, '45°', 'lb', 'end');\n    return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'The slope of the consumption function is the MPC, between 0 and 1: of each extra dollar of disposable income, part is spent and the rest is saved.' };\n  };\n\n  G.investment = function () {\n    var sx = scale(0, 10, X0, X1), sy = scale(0, 10, Y0, Y1);\n    var o = axes('I', 'r');\n    var f = function (x) { return 9.5 - 0.85 * x; };\n    o += curve(plot(f, 0.5, 10, sx, sy), 'c1');\n    o += dot(sx(3), sy(f(3))) + dot(sx(6.5), sy(f(6.5)));\n    o += hguide(sx(3), sy(f(3)), 'r₁') + vguide(sx(3), sy(f(3)), 'I₁');\n    o += hguide(sx(6.5), sy(f(6.5)), 'r₂') + vguide(sx(6.5), sy(f(6.5)), 'I₂');\n    o += t(sx(9.8), sy(f(9.8)) - 8, 'I(r)', 'k1', 'end');\n    return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'A higher real interest rate raises the cost of borrowing (or the return given up), so fewer projects pay off and investment falls.' };\n  };\n\n  G.loanableFunds = function (a) {\n    var sx = scale(0, 10, X0, X1), sy = scale(0, 10, Y0, Y1);\n    var f = function (x) { return 9.5 - 0.85 * x; };\n    var o = axes('S, I', 'r');\n    o += curve(plot(f, 0.5, 10, sx, sy), 'c1');\n    o += t(sx(9.8), sy(f(9.8)) - 8, 'I(r)', 'k1', 'end');\n    var s1 = 5.5;\n    if (!a.shift) {\n      o += ln(sx(s1), Y1, sx(s1), Y0, 'c2') + t(sx(s1) + 4, Y1 + 10, 'S = Y̅ − C − G', 'k2');\n      o += dot(sx(s1), sy(f(s1))) + hguide(sx(s1), sy(f(s1)), 'r*', 'k1');\n      return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'Saving does not depend on r here, so the supply of loanable funds is vertical. The real interest rate moves until investment demand equals it.' };\n    }\n    var s2 = a.shift === 'left' ? 3.6 : 7.2;\n    o += ln(sx(s1), Y1, sx(s1), Y0, 'c2 faint') + t(sx(s1) + 3, Y1 + 10, 'S₁', 'k2');\n    o += ln(sx(s2), Y1, sx(s2), Y0, 'c2') + t(sx(s2) + 3, Y1 + 10, 'S₂', 'k2');\n    o += curve([[sx(s1) - 2, sy(8.9)], [sx(s2) + (s2 < s1 ? 6 : -6), sy(8.9)]], 'c4', true);\n    o += dot(sx(s1), sy(f(s1)), 'solid4') + dot(sx(s2), sy(f(s2)));\n    o += hguide(sx(s1), sy(f(s1)), 'r₁') + hguide(sx(s2), sy(f(s2)), 'r₂', 'k1');\n    var cap = a.shift === 'left'\n      ? (a.cap || 'G rises (or T falls): public saving falls, S shifts left, r rises and investment falls by the same amount. That is crowding out.')\n      : (a.cap || 'T rises: public saving rises by ΔT, private saving falls by MPC·ΔT, so S shifts right by (1 − MPC)ΔT; r falls and investment rises.');\n    return { svg: svg(o), cap: cap };\n  };\n\n  // ------------------------------------------------------------ week 3\n\n  G.hyperinflation = function () {\n    var d = [['1914', 4.2], ['Jan 20', 65], ['Jul 22', 493], ['Jan 23', 17972], ['Jul 23', 353412], ['Sep 23', 9.89e7], ['Nov 23', 4.2e12]];\n    var sx = scale(0, d.length - 1, X0 + 16, X1 - 6), sy = scale(0, 13, Y0, Y1);\n    var o = axes('', 'marks per $ (log)');\n    [0, 3, 6, 9, 12].forEach(function (p) {\n      o += ln(X0, sy(p), X1, sy(p), 'grid') + t(X0 - 5, sy(p) + 4, p === 0 ? '1' : '10' + '<tspan dy=\"-5\" font-size=\"8\">' + p + '</tspan>', 'lb', 'end');\n    });\n    var pts = d.map(function (p, k) { return [sx(k), sy(Math.log(p[1]) / Math.LN10)]; });\n    o += curve(pts, 'c2');\n    pts.forEach(function (p, k) { o += dot(p[0], p[1], 'solid2') + t(p[0], Y0 + 14, d[k][0], 'lb', 'middle'); });\n    o += t(pts[6][0] - 6, pts[6][1] + 4, '4.2 trillion', 'k2', 'end');\n    return { svg: svg(o, 200), cap: 'German paper marks per US dollar. From 4.2 in 1914 to 4.2 trillion in November 1923: prices rose by roughly a trillion times.' };\n  };\n\n  G.moneyAggregates = function () {\n    var o = '';\n    o += t(16, 30, 'Monetary base', 'b') + t(16, 42, '(the Fed controls)', 'lb');\n    o += rect(120, 18, 70, 30, 'solid1', 6) + t(155, 37, 'currency', 'w', 'middle');\n    o += rect(192, 18, 110, 30, 'box1', 6) + t(247, 37, 'bank reserves', 'k1', 'middle');\n    o += t(16, 92, 'M1', 'b') + t(16, 104, '(spendable money)', 'lb');\n    o += rect(120, 80, 70, 30, 'solid1', 6) + t(155, 99, 'currency', 'w', 'middle');\n    o += rect(192, 80, 54, 30, 'box3', 6) + t(219, 93, 'check-', 'k3', 'middle') + t(219, 104, 'able', 'k3', 'middle');\n    o += rect(248, 80, 54, 30, 'box2', 6) + t(275, 93, 'savings', 'k2', 'middle') + t(275, 104, 'since ’20', 'k2', 'middle');\n    o += t(16, 152, 'M2', 'b') + t(16, 164, '= M1 + small time', 'lb') + t(16, 175, 'deposits + money funds', 'lb');\n    o += rect(120, 142, 182, 30, 'box', 6) + t(211, 161, 'broader still', 'lb', 'middle');\n    o += curve([[155, 50], [155, 76]], 'c4 dash', true);\n    return { svg: svg(o, 190), cap: 'Currency is in both. Reserves are in the base but not in M1; deposits are in M1 but not in the base. So the base is NOT \"all currency and deposits\".' };\n  };\n\n  G.quantityTheory = function () {\n    var sx = scale(0, 30, X0, X1), sy = scale(0, 30, Y0, Y1);\n    var pts = [[2, 1.5], [4, 2.8], [5, 4.2], [7, 5.1], [8, 7.5], [10, 8.2], [12, 10.5], [14, 13.6], [17, 15], [19, 18.5], [22, 19.6], [25, 24.1], [28, 26.5], [3, 3.6], [6, 3.4], [9, 6.2], [15, 12.4]];\n    var o = axes('money growth %ΔM', 'π %');\n    o += curve(plot(function (x) { return x - 2.5; }, 2.5, 30, sx, sy), 'c4 dash');\n    pts.forEach(function (p) { o += dot(sx(p[0]), sy(p[1]), 'solid1'); });\n    o += t(sx(29), sy(26.5) - 12, 'π = %ΔM − %ΔY', 'k1', 'end');\n    return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'Stylised cross-country picture: countries with faster money growth have higher inflation, about one for one, shifted down by real growth.' };\n  };\n\n  G.moneyDemand = function () {\n    var sx = scale(0, 10, X0, X1), sy = scale(0, 10, Y0, Y1);\n    var f = function (x) { return 9.6 / (0.5 + 0.3 * x) - 0.7; };\n    var o = axes('M/P', 'i');\n    o += curve(plot(f, 1.5, 10, sx, sy), 'c1');\n    o += ln(sx(5), Y1, sx(5), Y0, 'c2') + t(sx(5) + 4, Y1 + 10, '(M/P)ˢ', 'k2');\n    o += dot(sx(5), sy(f(5))) + hguide(sx(5), sy(f(5)), 'i*', 'k1');\n    o += t(sx(9.8), sy(f(9.8)) - 8, 'L(i, Y)', 'k1', 'end');\n    return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'Money pays no interest, so a higher nominal rate means you hold less of it: you spend it faster, and velocity rises.' };\n  };\n\n  G.fisher = function () {\n    var o = '';\n    var sy = scale(0, 8, 168, 20);\n    function bar(x, rr, pi, label) {\n      var s = rect(x, sy(rr), 64, sy(0) - sy(rr), 'solid3', 4);\n      s += rect(x, sy(rr + pi), 64, sy(rr) - sy(rr + pi), 'solid2', 4);\n      s += t(x + 32, sy(rr / 2) + 4, 'r = ' + rr, 'w', 'middle');\n      s += t(x + 32, sy(rr + pi / 2) + 4, 'π = ' + pi, 'w', 'middle');\n      s += t(x + 32, sy(rr + pi) - 6, 'i = ' + (rr + pi) + '%', 'b', 'middle');\n      s += t(x + 32, 184, label, 'lb', 'middle');\n      return s;\n    }\n    o += bar(60, 2, 3, 'before') + bar(196, 2, 5, 'expected π +2');\n    o += curve([[132, 90], [188, 70]], 'c4', true);\n    return { svg: svg(o, 192), cap: 'Fisher effect: when expected inflation rises 2 points, the nominal rate rises 2 points and the real rate (green) stays put.' };\n  };\n\n  G.inflationVsRates = function () {\n    var o = '';\n    var rows = [['Fed funds target', '3.75–4.00%', 'Sep 16, 2026'], ['CPI inflation, 12 mo.', '3.4%', 'Aug 2026'], ['Real policy rate ≈', '+0.5%', 'i − π']];\n    rows.forEach(function (row, k) {\n      var y = 26 + k * 50;\n      o += rect(16, y - 16, 288, 40, k === 2 ? 'box1' : 'box', 10);\n      o += t(30, y + 4, row[0], k === 2 ? 'k1' : 'b');\n      o += t(290, y + 4, row[1], k === 2 ? 'k1' : 'b', 'end');\n      o += t(30, y + 17, row[2], 'lb');\n    });\n    return { svg: svg(o, 176), cap: 'Using ex post inflation. With expected inflation in place of actual, you get the ex ante real rate instead.' };\n  };\n\n  // ------------------------------------------------------------ week 4\n\n  G.flows = function () {\n    var o = '';\n    o += '<circle cx=\"78\" cy=\"96\" r=\"50\" class=\"box3\"/>' + t(78, 92, 'Employed', 'k3', 'middle') + t(78, 108, 'E', 'it', 'middle');\n    o += '<circle cx=\"242\" cy=\"96\" r=\"50\" class=\"box2\"/>' + t(242, 92, 'Unemployed', 'k2', 'middle') + t(242, 108, 'U', 'it', 'middle');\n    o += curve([[118, 66], [160, 40], [204, 66]], 'c2', true);\n    o += t(160, 32, 'job losses  s · E', 'k2', 'middle');\n    o += curve([[204, 126], [160, 152], [118, 126]], 'c3', true);\n    o += t(160, 170, 'job finding  f · U', 'k3', 'middle');\n    o += t(160, 194, 'steady state: s·E = f·U  ⇒  U/L = s/(s + f)', 'it', 'middle');\n    return { svg: svg(o, 202), cap: 'Each period a share s of the employed lose jobs and a share f of the unemployed find one. When the two flows match, unemployment stops changing.' };\n  };\n\n  G.unemploymentPath = function () {\n    var s = 0.02, f = 0.28, u = 0.12, pts = [];\n    var sx = scale(0, 14, X0, X1), sy = scale(0, 13, Y0, Y1);\n    for (var k = 0; k <= 14; k++) { pts.push([sx(k), sy(u * 100)]); u = u + s * (1 - u) - f * u; }\n    var o = axes('months', 'u %');\n    var ss = s / (s + f) * 100;\n    o += ln(X0, sy(ss), X1, sy(ss), 'c2 dash') + t(X1, sy(ss) - 6, 'u* = 6.67%', 'k2', 'end');\n    o += curve(pts, 'c1');\n    pts.forEach(function (p) { o += dot(p[0], p[1], 'solid1'); });\n    o += t(X0 - 5, sy(12) + 4, '12', 'lb', 'end');\n    return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'Start at 12% with s = 0.02 and f = 0.28: unemployment falls quickly toward s/(s + f) = 6.67% and stays there.' };\n  };\n\n  G.wageFloor = function (a) {\n    var sx = scale(0, 10, X0, X1), sy = scale(0, 10, Y0, Y1);\n    var D = function (x) { return 9.2 - 0.8 * x; }, S = function (x) { return 1 + 0.8 * x; };\n    var o = axes('labor', 'W/P');\n    o += curve(plot(D, 0.5, 10, sx, sy), 'c1') + t(sx(9.9), sy(D(9.9)) - 8, 'demand', 'k1', 'end');\n    o += curve(plot(S, 0, 10, sx, sy), 'c3') + t(sx(9.9), sy(S(9.9)) + 14, 'supply', 'k3', 'end');\n    var w = 7.2, ld = (9.2 - w) / 0.8, ls = (w - 1) / 0.8;\n    o += ln(X0, sy(w), X1, sy(w), 'c2') + t(X0 + 4, sy(w) - 6, a.label || 'wage floor', 'k2');\n    o += dot(sx(ld), sy(w)) + dot(sx(ls), sy(w));\n    o += ln(sx(ld), sy(w) + 14, sx(ls), sy(w) + 14, 'c2', true);\n    o += t((sx(ld) + sx(ls)) / 2, sy(w) + 28, 'unemployed', 'k2', 'middle');\n    o += dot(sx(5.125), sy(D(5.125)), 'solid4');\n    return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'With the real wage held above where supply meets demand, more people want work than firms want to hire. The gap is structural unemployment.' };\n  };\n\n  // ------------------------------------------------------------ week 5\n\n  G.solow = function (a) {\n    var mode = a.mode || 'basic';\n    var alpha = 0.5, s = 0.3, d = 0.1, n = 0.04;\n    var f = function (k) { return Math.pow(k, alpha); };\n    // The golden-rule picture needs room for k_gold = 25 and f(k) itself.\n    var kmax = mode === 'golden' ? 40 : 14;\n    var sx = scale(0, kmax, X0, X1), sy = scale(0, mode === 'golden' ? 7 : 1.25, Y0, Y1);\n    // Straight lines stop where they reach the top of the plot.\n    var kTop = mode === 'golden' ? kmax * 0.95 : Math.min(kmax * 0.95, 1.2 / d);\n    var kss = function (sv, dd) { return Math.pow(sv / dd, 1 / (1 - alpha)); };\n    var o = axes('k', '');\n    var cap;\n    if (mode === 'basic' || mode === 'diagram') {\n      o += curve(plot(function (k) { return s * f(k); }, 0, kmax, sx, sy, 90), 'c1') + t(sx(kmax), sy(s * f(kmax)) + 14, 's f(k)', 'k1', 'end');\n      o += curve(plot(function (k) { return d * k; }, 0, kTop, sx, sy), 'c2') + t(sx(kTop) - 6, sy(d * kTop) + 4, 'δk', 'k2', 'end');\n      var k1 = kss(s, d);\n      o += dot(sx(k1), sy(d * k1)) + vguide(sx(k1), sy(d * k1), 'k*', 'k1');\n      o += curve([[sx(2.4), sy(0.36)], [sx(5.4), sy(0.36)]], 'c4', true) + t(sx(2.4), sy(0.36) - 6, 'k rises', 'lb');\n      o += curve([[sx(13), sy(0.62)], [sx(10.4), sy(0.62)]], 'c4', true) + t(sx(13), sy(0.62) - 6, 'k falls', 'lb', 'end');\n      cap = mode === 'diagram'\n        ? 'Label everything: axes (k, and investment/depreciation per worker), s f(k), δk (and f(k) above them if you draw it), the crossing k*, and arrows showing k moving toward it from both sides.'\n        : 'Left of k*, investment s f(k) beats depreciation δk and k grows; right of it, depreciation wins. They meet at the steady state.';\n    } else if (mode === 'saving') {\n      var s2 = 0.4;\n      o += curve(plot(function (k) { return s * f(k); }, 0, kmax, sx, sy, 90), 'c1 faint') + t(sx(kmax), sy(s * f(kmax)) + 14, 's₁ f(k)', 'k1', 'end');\n      o += curve(plot(function (k) { return s2 * f(k); }, 0, kmax, sx, sy, 90), 'c1') + t(sx(kmax), sy(s2 * f(kmax)) - 6, 's₂ f(k)', 'k1', 'end');\n      o += curve(plot(function (k) { return d * k; }, 0, kTop, sx, sy), 'c2');\n      var a1 = kss(s, d), a2 = kss(s2, d);\n      o += dot(sx(a1), sy(d * a1), 'solid4') + vguide(sx(a1), sy(d * a1), 'k₁*');\n      o += dot(sx(a2), sy(d * a2)) + vguide(sx(a2), sy(d * a2), 'k₂*', 'k1');\n      cap = 'A higher saving rate lifts s f(k): the steady state moves right, so income per worker ends up higher, but growth stops again once k reaches k₂*.';\n    } else if (mode === 'pop') {\n      o += curve(plot(function (k) { return s * f(k); }, 0, kmax, sx, sy, 90), 'c1') + t(sx(kmax), sy(s * f(kmax)) + 14, 's f(k)', 'k1', 'end');\n      o += curve(plot(function (k) { return d * k; }, 0, kTop, sx, sy), 'c2 faint') + t(sx(kTop) - 6, sy(d * kTop) + 4, 'δk', 'k2', 'end');\n      o += curve(plot(function (k) { return (d + n) * k; }, 0, 8.6, sx, sy), 'c2') + t(sx(8.6), sy((d + n) * 8.6) - 6, '(δ + n)k', 'k2', 'end');\n      var b1 = kss(s, d), b2 = kss(s, d + n);\n      o += dot(sx(b1), sy(d * b1), 'solid4') + vguide(sx(b1), sy(d * b1), 'k*');\n      o += dot(sx(b2), sy((d + n) * b2)) + vguide(sx(b2), sy((d + n) * b2), 'k*ₙ', 'k2');\n      cap = 'Population growth n is like extra depreciation: new workers need capital too. The break-even line steepens and steady-state k falls.';\n    } else {\n      var kg = Math.pow(alpha / d, 1 / (1 - alpha));\n      o += curve(plot(f, 0, kmax, sx, sy, 90), 'c1') + t(sx(kmax), sy(f(kmax)) - 6, 'f(k)', 'k1', 'end');\n      o += curve(plot(function (k) { return d * k; }, 0, kmax * 0.95, sx, sy), 'c2') + t(sx(kmax * 0.95), sy(d * kmax * 0.95) + 14, 'δk', 'k2', 'end');\n      o += ln(sx(kg), sy(f(kg)), sx(kg), sy(d * kg), 'c3', false);\n      o += t(sx(kg) + 5, sy((f(kg) + d * kg) / 2), 'c* biggest', 'k3');\n      o += ln(sx(kg - 3), sy(f(kg) - 3 * d), sx(kg + 3), sy(f(kg) + 3 * d), 'c3 dash');\n      o += vguide(sx(kg), sy(d * kg), 'k_gold', 'k3');\n      cap = 'Consumption per worker is the gap between f(k) and δk. It is widest where the slope of f(k) equals δ, i.e. MPK = δ.';\n    }\n    return { svg: svg(o), cap: cap };\n  };\n\n  G.solowShock = function (a) {\n    var v = a.v || 'y';\n    var sx = scale(0, 10, X0, X1), sy = scale(0, 10, Y0, Y1);\n    var o = axes('time', v === 'c' ? 'c' : v === 'Y' ? 'Y (total)' : 'y = Y/L');\n    var base = 4.2, t0 = 3;\n    var path = function (x) { return base + 2.6 * Math.exp(-(x - t0) * 0.55); };\n    if (v === 'Y') {\n      var Yp = function (x) { return 4.2 + 0.8 * Math.exp(-(x - t0) * 0.55); };\n      o += curve([[sx(0), sy(6.4)], [sx(t0), sy(6.4)]], 'c1');\n      o += ln(sx(t0), sy(6.4), sx(t0), sy(Yp(t0)), 'c1 dash');\n      o += curve(plot(Yp, t0, 10, sx, sy), 'c1');\n      o += t(sx(t0), Y0 + 14, 'L falls', 'k2', 'middle');\n      o += t(sx(t0) + 6, sy(5.9), 'Y drops with L,', 'k1');\n      o += t(sx(t0) + 6, sy(5.3), 'then K shrinks too', 'lb');\n      return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'Total output Y falls: fewer workers, and over time less capital. Per-worker output only rises for a while.' };\n    }\n    o += curve([[sx(0), sy(base)], [sx(t0), sy(base)]], 'c1');\n    o += ln(sx(t0), sy(base), sx(t0), sy(base + 2.6), 'c1 dash');\n    o += curve(plot(path, t0, 10, sx, sy), 'c1');\n    o += ln(X0, sy(base), X1, sy(base), 'c4 dash faint');\n    o += t(sx(t0), Y0 + 14, 'L falls', 'k2', 'middle');\n    o += t(sx(9.8), sy(base) - 6, 'same steady state', 'lb', 'end');\n    o += t(sx(t0) + 6, sy(base + 2.5), v === 'c' ? 'c jumps up' : 'k and y jump up', 'k1');\n    o += t(sx(4.6), sy(base + 1.3), 'then falls back', 'lb');\n    return { svg: svg(o), cap: v === 'c'\n      ? 'With s unchanged, c = (1 − s)y moves with y: it jumps up, then falls back to where it started.'\n      : 'Fewer workers share the same capital, so k = K/L jumps above k*. Depreciation then outruns investment and k and y slide back to the old steady state.' };\n  };\n\n  G.savingPath = function () {\n    var sx = scale(0, 10, X0, X1), sy = scale(0, 10, Y0, Y1);\n    var t0 = 3;\n    var o = axes('time', 'y');\n    o += curve(plot(function (x) { return x < t0 ? 3 : 3 + 3.5 * (1 - Math.exp(-(x - t0) * 0.5)); }, 0, 10, sx, sy, 80), 'c1');\n    o += ln(X0, sy(6.5), X1, sy(6.5), 'c4 dash faint');\n    o += t(sx(t0), Y0 + 14, 's rises', 'k2', 'middle');\n    o += t(sx(9.8), sy(6.5) - 6, 'higher level', 'k1', 'end');\n    o += t(sx(4.6), sy(3.6), 'faster growth', 'lb');\n    o += t(sx(4.6), sy(2.8), 'only for a while', 'lb');\n    return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'A permanent rise in the saving rate gives a burst of growth and a permanently higher level of y, not a permanently higher growth rate.' };\n  };\n\n  G.goldenHump = function () {\n    var alpha = 0.33;\n    var sx = scale(0, 1, X0, X1), sy = scale(0, 1.05, Y0, Y1);\n    var c = function (s) { return s <= 0 || s >= 1 ? 0 : (1 - s) * Math.pow(s, alpha / (1 - alpha)) / Math.pow(alpha, alpha / (1 - alpha)) / (1 - alpha); };\n    var o = axes('saving rate s', 'c*');\n    o += curve(plot(c, 0.001, 0.999, sx, sy, 120), 'c1');\n    o += dot(sx(alpha), sy(c(alpha))) + vguide(sx(alpha), sy(c(alpha)), 's_gold = α', 'k3');\n    o += t(sx(0.2), sy(0.22), 'below: save more', 'lb', 'middle');\n    o += t(sx(0.73), sy(0.92), 'above: save less', 'lb', 'middle');\n    return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'Steady-state consumption peaks when the saving rate equals capital’s share α. Saving 0% or 100% both leave nothing to eat.' };\n  };\n\n  G.convergence = function () {\n    var sx = scale(0, 10, X0, X1), sy = scale(0, 10, Y0, Y1);\n    var o = axes('time', 'y');\n    o += curve(plot(function (x) { return 8.4 - 0.9 * Math.exp(-x * 0.4); }, 0, 10, sx, sy), 'c4');\n    o += curve(plot(function (x) { return 8.4 - 6.5 * Math.exp(-x * 0.4); }, 0, 10, sx, sy), 'c1');\n    o += t(sx(0.2), sy(7.5) - 8, 'rich: near steady state', 'lb');\n    o += t(sx(0.4), sy(1.9) + 16, 'war-damaged: grows fast', 'k1');\n    o += ln(X0, sy(8.4), X1, sy(8.4), 'c4 dash faint');\n    return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'An economy far below its steady state has a high return on capital and grows fast; growth slows as it closes the gap (postwar Japan, Germany).' };\n  };\n\n  G.chinaGrowth = function () {\n    var d = [[2010, 10.6], [2012, 7.9], [2014, 7.4], [2016, 6.8], [2018, 6.7], [2019, 6.0], [2020, 2.2], [2021, 8.4], [2022, 3.0], [2023, 5.2], [2024, 5.0], [2025, 5.0]];\n    var sx = scale(-0.6, d.length - 0.4, X0, X1), sy = scale(0, 12, Y0, Y1);\n    var o = axes('', '%');\n    [4, 8, 12].forEach(function (v) { o += ln(X0, sy(v), X1, sy(v), 'grid') + t(X0 - 5, sy(v) + 4, v, 'lb', 'end'); });\n    d.forEach(function (p, k) {\n      o += rect(sx(k) - 8, sy(p[1]), 16, Y0 - sy(p[1]), 'solid1', 3);\n      o += t(sx(k), Y0 + 13, \"'\" + String(p[0]).slice(2), 'lb', 'middle');\n    });\n    o += t(sx(0), sy(10.6) - 5, '10.6', 'k1', 'middle') + t(sx(11), sy(5) - 5, '5.0', 'k1', 'middle');\n    return { svg: svg(o, 196), cap: 'China’s official real GDP growth (NBS). From double digits to about 5%; outside estimates put recent years lower still.' };\n  };\n\n  G.crs = function () {\n    var o = '';\n    function grid(x, n, cls) {\n      var s = '';\n      for (var k = 0; k < n; k++) s += rect(x + (k % 2) * 26, 30 + Math.floor(k / 2) * 26, 22, 22, cls, 4);\n      return s;\n    }\n    o += t(60, 20, 'K, L', 'b', 'middle') + grid(34, 2, 'solid1');\n    o += t(60, 100, 'Y', 'b', 'middle') + rect(40, 108, 40, 22, 'solid2', 4);\n    o += curve([[110, 70], [190, 70]], 'c4', true) + t(150, 62, '× 2', 'b', 'middle');\n    o += t(250, 20, '2K, 2L', 'b', 'middle') + grid(224, 4, 'solid1');\n    o += t(250, 100, '2Y', 'b', 'middle') + rect(206, 108, 40, 22, 'solid2', 4) + rect(252, 108, 40, 22, 'solid2', 4);\n    o += t(160, 160, 'F(zK, zL) = z · F(K, L)', 'it', 'middle');\n    return { svg: svg(o, 172), cap: 'Double every input, get exactly double the output. Cobb-Douglas has this because the exponents α and 1 − α add to 1.' };\n  };\n\n  G.wageCompression = function () {\n    var sx = scale(0, 10, X0, X1), sy = scale(0, 10, Y0, Y1);\n    var o = axes('wage percentile', 'wage growth');\n    var pts = [[1, 8.6], [2.5, 7.4], [4, 6.2], [5.5, 5.3], [7, 4.6], [8.5, 4.1], [9.6, 3.8]];\n    o += curve(pts.map(function (p) { return [sx(p[0]), sy(p[1])]; }), 'c1');\n    pts.forEach(function (p) { o += dot(sx(p[0]), sy(p[1]), 'solid1'); });\n    o += t(sx(1), Y0 + 14, '10th', 'lb', 'middle') + t(sx(5.5), Y0 + 14, '50th', 'lb', 'middle') + t(sx(9.6), Y0 + 14, '90th', 'lb', 'middle');\n    o += t(sx(1.4), sy(8.6) - 8, 'fastest at the bottom', 'k1');\n    return { svg: svg(o), cap: 'Stylised. In the tight post-pandemic labor market, wages rose fastest for the lowest paid, narrowing the gap with the middle and top.' };\n  };\n\n  window.GRAPHS = G;\n  // The drawing helpers, for the other classes' graph files (tools/graphs/<id>.js),\n  // which add their own entries to window.GRAPHS in the same style.\n  window.GRAPHKIT = { svg: svg, t: t, ln: ln, dot: dot, rect: rect, r: r, pathD: pathD, curve: curve, area: area,\n    plot: plot, scale: scale, axes: axes, vguide: vguide, hguide: hguide, W: W, H: H, X0: X0, Y0: Y0, X1: X1, Y1: Y1 };\n})();\n\n// IHUM 242 card graphs, added to the shared library (tools/graphs.js) with\n// its drawing helpers (window.GRAPHKIT). Same style as the ECON 381 graphs:\n// unit colour (c1/k1/box1/solid1), orange (c2/k2/box2), green (c3/k3/box3),\n// ink (c4, box, solid4).\n//\n// Plain ES5 on purpose: no backticks, no \"${\".\n(function () {\n  var K = window.GRAPHKIT, G = window.GRAPHS;\n  var svg = K.svg, t = K.t, ln = K.ln, dot = K.dot, rect = K.rect, curve = K.curve, area = K.area, scale = K.scale;\n\n  function has(list, v) {\n    if (!list) return false;\n    if (typeof list === 'string') return list === v;\n    for (var k = 0; k < list.length; k++) if (list[k] === v) return true;\n    return false;\n  }\n  function arrow(x1, y1, x2, y2, cls) { return ln(x1, y1, x2, y2, cls || 'c4', true); }\n\n  // ------------------------------------------------------- dynasty timeline\n  // Same date ranges as the Timeline tool (classes/ihum242.js, DYN).\n  var DYN = [\n    ['Rashidun', 'Rashidun', 632, 661], ['Umayyad', 'Umayyad', 661, 750], ['Abbasid', 'Abbasid', 750, 1258],\n    ['Umayyads of Córdoba', 'Umayyad, Spain', 756, 1031], ['Fatimid', 'Fatimid', 909, 1171],\n    ['Great Seljuk', 'Great Seljuk', 1037, 1194], ['Ayyubid', 'Ayyubid', 1171, 1250], ['Mamluk', 'Mamluk', 1250, 1517],\n    ['Nasrid', 'Nasrid', 1232, 1492], ['Ilkhanate', 'Ilkhanate', 1256, 1335],\n    ['Ottoman', 'Ottoman', 1299, 1922], ['Safavid', 'Safavid', 1501, 1736], ['Mughal', 'Mughal', 1526, 1857],\n  ];\n  G.ihum242Dyn = DYN;\n\n  G.ihum242Timeline = function (a) {\n    a = a || {};\n    var sx = scale(600, 1950, 98, 306), rowH = 14.5, top = 10;\n    var o = '';\n    [700, 1000, 1300, 1600, 1900].forEach(function (y) {\n      o += ln(sx(y), top - 4, sx(y), top + DYN.length * rowH + 2, 'c4 dash faint');\n      o += t(sx(y), top + DYN.length * rowH + 14, String(y), 'lb', 'middle');\n    });\n    DYN.forEach(function (d, k) {\n      var y = top + k * rowH, on = has(a.hi, d[0]);\n      var x0 = sx(d[2]), w = Math.max(3, sx(d[3]) - sx(d[2]));\n      o += rect(x0, y + 2, w, rowH - 5, on ? 'solid1' : 'solid4', 2);\n      o += t(92, y + rowH - 4, d[1], on ? 'k1' : 'lb', 'end');\n      if (on) {\n        var lab = d[2] + '–' + d[3];\n        // The dates go after the bar, or before it when the bar runs to the edge.\n        o += sx(d[3]) + 52 < 312 ? t(sx(d[3]) + 4, y + rowH - 4, lab, 'k1', 'start') : t(x0 - 4, y + rowH - 4, lab, 'k1', 'end');\n      }\n    });\n    var cap = a.cap || 'Major dynasties, 632–1922 CE. Several overlap: after c. 950 the Abbasid caliph in Baghdad reigned while regional dynasties ruled.';\n    return { svg: svg(o, top + DYN.length * rowH + 20), cap: cap };\n  };\n\n  // ------------------------------------------------------- schematic map\n  var CITIES = [\n    // name, lon, lat, label dx, label dy, anchor\n    ['Mecca', 39.83, 21.42, -6, 4, 'end'], ['Medina', 39.61, 24.47, -6, 4, 'end'],\n    ['Jerusalem', 35.22, 31.78, -6, 6, 'end'], ['Damascus', 36.29, 33.51, -6, -1, 'end'],\n    ['Baghdad', 44.37, 33.31, 0, -7, 'middle'], ['Cairo', 31.24, 30.04, 0, 13, 'middle'],\n    ['Córdoba', -4.78, 37.89, 6, 4, 'start'], ['Fez', -5.0, 34.03, 6, 4, 'start'],\n    ['Istanbul', 28.98, 41.01, 6, 3, 'start'],\n    ['Konya', 32.49, 37.87, -6, 4, 'end'], ['Isfahan', 51.67, 32.65, 6, 5, 'start'],\n    ['Samarkand', 66.96, 39.65, 0, -7, 'middle'], ['Delhi', 77.21, 28.61, -6, 6, 'end'],\n  ];\n  G.ihum242Cities = CITIES;\n\n  G.ihum242Map = function (a) {\n    a = a || {};\n    var sx = scale(-10, 80, 12, 308), sy = scale(19, 44, 188, 10);\n    var o = rect(6, 2, 308, 192, 'box', 8);\n    [['Iberia', -4, 42], ['N. Africa', 3, 30.5], ['Arabia', 50, 20], ['Iran', 57, 37], ['India', 72, 21.5]].forEach(function (r) {\n      o += t(sx(r[1]), sy(r[2]), r[0], 'it faint', 'middle');\n    });\n    if (a.route) {\n      var A = CITIES[0], B = CITIES[1];\n      o += curve([[sx(A[1]) + 4, sy(A[2]) - 3], [sx(A[1]) + 13, sy((A[2] + B[2]) / 2)], [sx(B[1]) + 5, sy(B[2]) + 3]], 'c4', true);\n      o += t(sx(A[1]) + 18, sy((A[2] + B[2]) / 2) + 4, 'hijra, 622', 'k2', 'start');\n    }\n    CITIES.forEach(function (c) {\n      var on = !a.hi || has(a.hi, c[0]);\n      var x = sx(c[1]), y = sy(c[2]);\n      o += '<circle cx=\"' + K.r(x) + '\" cy=\"' + K.r(y) + '\" r=\"' + (on && a.hi ? 4 : 3) + '\" class=\"' + (on ? (a.hi ? 'solid1' : 'dot') : 'solid4') + '\"/>';\n      o += t(x + c[3], y + c[4], c[0], on ? (a.hi ? 'k1' : '') : 'lb', c[5]);\n    });\n    return { svg: svg(o, 196), cap: a.cap || 'Schematic, not to scale: key cities of the classical Islamic world placed by longitude and latitude (10° W to 80° E).' };\n  };\n\n  // ------------------------------------------------------- succession\n  G.ihum242Succession = function (a) {\n    a = a || {};\n    var sOn = a.hi !== 'shia', hOn = a.hi !== 'sunni';\n    var o = rect(95, 6, 130, 28, 'box', 10) + t(160, 24, 'Muhammad (d. 632)', 'b', 'middle');\n    o += curve([[160, 34], [160, 42], [82, 42], [82, 52]], 'c4', true);\n    o += curve([[160, 42], [240, 42], [240, 52]], 'c4', true);\n    o += t(82, 64, 'Sunni view', sOn ? 'k1' : 'lb', 'middle') + t(240, 64, 'Shi‘i view', hOn ? 'k2' : 'lb', 'middle');\n    var L = [['Abu Bakr', '632–634'], ['‘Umar', '634–644'], ['‘Uthman', '644–656'], ['‘Ali', '656–661']];\n    var R = [['‘Ali', 'designated by Muhammad'], ['Hasan', '2nd Imam'], ['Husayn', 'killed at Karbala, 680'], ['Imams of his line', 'Twelver: 12th hidden 874']];\n    for (var k = 0; k < 4; k++) {\n      var y = 72 + k * 40;\n      o += rect(14, y, 136, 26, sOn ? 'box1' : 'box', 7) + t(22, y + 17, L[k][0], sOn ? 'k1' : 'b', 'start') + t(142, y + 17, L[k][1], 'lb', 'end');\n      o += rect(170, y, 140, 26, hOn ? 'box2' : 'box', 7) + t(178, y + 12, R[k][0], hOn ? 'k2' : 'b', 'start') + t(178, y + 23, R[k][1], 'lb', 'start');\n      if (k < 3) { o += arrow(82, y + 27, 82, y + 38) + arrow(298, y + 27, 298, y + 38); }\n    }\n    o += t(82, 232, 'community’s choice; all four', 'lb', 'middle') + t(82, 243, '“rightly guided” (Rashidun)', 'lb', 'middle');\n    o += t(240, 232, 'leadership (imamate) belongs', 'lb', 'middle') + t(240, 243, 'to ‘Ali and his descendants', 'lb', 'middle');\n    return { svg: svg(o, 248), cap: 'Both sides accept the same four men as historical caliphs; they differ on whether ‘Ali should have led first and on who holds religious authority after him.' };\n  };\n\n  // ------------------------------------------------------- sources of law\n  G.ihum242LawLadder = function (a) {\n    a = a || {};\n    var rows = [['quran', 'Qur’an', 'revealed text'], ['sunna', 'Sunna', 'Prophet’s practice, known via hadith'],\n      ['ijma', 'Ijma‘', 'consensus of qualified scholars'], ['qiyas', 'Qiyas', 'analogy from a known ruling']];\n    var o = '';\n    rows.forEach(function (r, k) {\n      var x = 14 + k * 22, y = 10 + k * 40, on = !a.hi || a.hi === r[0];\n      o += rect(x, y, 200, 32, on ? 'box1' : 'box', 8);\n      o += t(x + 10, y + 14, (k + 1) + '. ' + r[1], on ? 'k1' : 'b', 'start') + t(x + 10, y + 26, r[2], 'lb', 'start');\n      if (k < 3) o += curve([[x + 200, y + 16], [x + 214, y + 16], [x + 214, y + 38]], 'c4', true);\n    });\n    o += rect(14, 172, 296, 56, 'box2', 8);\n    o += t(22, 188, 'Fiqh = the jurist’s reasoning (ijtihad) on these', 'k2', 'start');\n    o += t(22, 203, 'No clear answer at one rung? Go down to the next.', '', 'start');\n    o += t(22, 218, 'Twelver Shi‘i usul: Qur’an, Sunna (incl. Imams), ijma‘, ‘aql', 'lb', 'start');\n    return { svg: svg(o, 232), cap: 'The classical Sunni sources of law (usul al-fiqh), as systematized after al-Shafi‘i (d. 820). Higher sources control the lower.' };\n  };\n\n  // ------------------------------------------------------- five pillars\n  G.ihum242Pillars = function (a) {\n    a = a || {};\n    var P = [['shahada', 'Shahada', 'witness', 'of faith'], ['salat', 'Salat', 'prayer', '5 daily'], ['zakat', 'Zakat', 'alms', '2.5%'],\n      ['sawm', 'Sawm', 'fasting', 'Ramadan'], ['hajj', 'Hajj', 'to Mecca', 'if able']];\n    var o = area([[14, 50], [160, 10], [306, 50]], 'fill1') + curve([[14, 50], [160, 10], [306, 50]], 'c1');\n    o += t(160, 40, 'Islam (“submission”)', 'k1', 'middle');\n    o += rect(14, 52, 292, 8, 'solid4', 2);\n    P.forEach(function (p, k) {\n      var x = 17 + k * 58, on = !a.hi || a.hi === p[0];\n      o += rect(x, 64, 52, 100, on ? 'box1' : 'box', 4);\n      o += t(x + 26, 92, p[1], on ? 'k1' : 'b', 'middle');\n      o += t(x + 26, 112, p[2], 'lb', 'middle') + t(x + 26, 126, p[3], 'lb', 'middle');\n    });\n    o += rect(14, 168, 292, 8, 'solid4', 2);\n    return { svg: svg(o, 182), cap: 'The five ritual duties shared by Sunnis and Shi‘is (Shi‘i theology groups the obligations a little differently).' };\n  };\n\n  // ------------------------------------------------------- madhhabs\n  G.ihum242Madhhabs = function (a) {\n    a = a || {};\n    var M = [['hanafi', 'Hanafi', 'Abu Hanifa (d. 767)', 'Turkey, C. & S. Asia'],\n      ['maliki', 'Maliki', 'Malik (d. 795)', 'North & West Africa'],\n      ['shafii', 'Shafi‘i', 'al-Shafi‘i (d. 820)', 'Egypt, E. Africa, SE Asia'],\n      ['hanbali', 'Hanbali', 'Ibn Hanbal (d. 855)', 'Arabian Peninsula']];\n    var o = t(160, 14, 'Sunni schools of law', 'b', 'middle');\n    M.forEach(function (m, k) {\n      var x = 14 + (k % 2) * 150, y = 22 + Math.floor(k / 2) * 64, on = !a.hi || a.hi === m[0];\n      o += rect(x, y, 142, 56, on ? 'box1' : 'box', 8);\n      o += t(x + 8, y + 17, m[1], on ? 'k1' : 'b', 'start');\n      o += t(x + 8, y + 32, m[2], '', 'start') + t(x + 8, y + 47, m[3], 'lb', 'start');\n    });\n    var on = !a.hi || a.hi === 'jafari';\n    o += rect(14, 156, 292, 40, on ? 'box2' : 'box', 8);\n    o += t(22, 172, 'Shi‘i (Twelver): Ja‘fari', on ? 'k2' : 'b', 'start') + t(22, 188, 'after Imam Ja‘far al-Sadiq (d. 765); Iran, Iraq', 'lb', 'start');\n    return { svg: svg(o, 202), cap: 'Each school is a method and body of rulings, not a sect: Sunnis regard all four as legitimate. Regions are where each predominates today.' };\n  };\n\n  // ------------------------------------------------------- isnad / silsila\n  G.ihum242Isnad = function (a) {\n    a = a || {};\n    var sil = a.mode === 'silsila';\n    var chain = sil\n      ? [['the Prophet', 'source of the path'], ['‘Ali (or Abu Bakr)', 'first link'], ['founding shaykh', 'e.g. al-Jilani, Naqshband'], ['your shaykh', 'living master'], ['disciple (murid)', 'initiated into the order']]\n      : [['the Prophet', 'said, did or approved'], ['Companion', 'saw or heard it'], ['Successor', 'heard it from him'], ['later transmitters', '“X told me that Y said…”'], ['compiler', 'e.g. al-Bukhari (d. 870)']];\n    var o = t(84, 14, sil ? 'Silsila (chain)' : 'Isnad (chain)', 'k1', 'middle');\n    chain.forEach(function (c, k) {\n      var y = 22 + k * 40;\n      o += rect(14, y, 140, 27, 'box1', 8) + t(84, y + 12, c[0], 'b', 'middle') + t(84, y + 23, c[1], 'lb', 'middle');\n      if (k < chain.length - 1) o += arrow(84, y + 28, 84, y + 39);\n    });\n    if (sil) {\n      o += rect(176, 70, 134, 84, 'box2', 8);\n      o += t(243, 90, 'What passes down', 'k2', 'middle') + t(243, 106, 'blessing (baraka),', 'lb', 'middle');\n      o += t(243, 119, 'the order’s dhikr,', 'lb', 'middle') + t(243, 132, 'the right to guide', 'lb', 'middle');\n      o += arrow(176, 112, 158, 112);\n    } else {\n      o += rect(176, 58, 134, 70, 'box2', 8);\n      o += t(243, 76, 'Matn (text)', 'k2', 'middle') + t(243, 93, 'the report itself:', 'lb', 'middle') + t(243, 106, 'what was said', 'lb', 'middle') + t(243, 119, 'or done', 'lb', 'middle');\n      o += rect(176, 140, 134, 58, 'box', 8);\n      o += t(243, 157, 'Critics grade it', 'b', 'middle') + t(243, 172, 'sahih (sound), hasan', 'lb', 'middle') + t(243, 185, '(good), da‘if (weak)', 'lb', 'middle');\n      o += arrow(176, 92, 158, 92);\n    }\n    return { svg: svg(o, 214), cap: sil ? 'A Sufi order traces its teaching master to master back to the Prophet, as a hadith traces its words.'\n      : 'A hadith = isnad + matn. Scholars judged a report largely by its chain: were the transmitters reliable, and could they have met?' };\n  };\n\n  // ------------------------------------------------------- translation\n  G.ihum242Translation = function () {\n    var o = '';\n    var B = [['Greek', 'Aristotle, Galen, Ptolemy, Euclid', 'box'], ['Syriac', 'Christian scholars, often the bridge', 'box'],\n      ['Arabic', 'Abbasid Baghdad, 8th–10th c.', 'box1'], ['Latin', 'Toledo, 12th c.; Gerard of Cremona', 'box3']];\n    B.forEach(function (b, k) {\n      var y = 8 + k * 48;\n      o += rect(14, y, 186, 36, b[2], 8) + t(24, y + 15, b[0], k === 2 ? 'k1' : (k === 3 ? 'k3' : 'b'), 'start') + t(24, y + 29, b[1], 'lb', 'start');\n      if (k < 3) o += arrow(107, y + 37, 107, y + 47);\n    });\n    o += curve([[200, 26], [214, 26], [214, 110], [203, 110]], 'c4 dash', true);\n    o += t(218, 62, 'some texts', 'lb', 'start') + t(218, 74, 'went straight', 'lb', 'start') + t(218, 86, 'Greek→Arabic', 'lb', 'start');\n    o += rect(226, 120, 84, 46, 'box2', 8) + t(268, 136, 'Persian &', 'k2', 'middle') + t(268, 148, 'Indian works,', 'lb', 'middle') + t(268, 160, 'numerals', 'lb', 'middle');\n    o += arrow(226, 130, 203, 130);\n    o += t(160, 202, 'Arabic scholars added commentary and new work, not just copies.', 'lb', 'middle');\n    return { svg: svg(o, 208), cap: 'The translation movement: Greek learning, often through Syriac, into Arabic under the Abbasids, and later from Arabic into Latin.' };\n  };\n\n  // ------------------------------------------------------- falsafa vs kalam\n  G.ihum242FalsafaKalam = function () {\n    var o = '';\n    o += rect(14, 8, 140, 112, 'box1', 8) + t(84, 26, 'Falsafa', 'k1', 'middle');\n    o += t(84, 42, 'starts from Greek', 'lb', 'middle') + t(84, 54, 'demonstrative reason', 'lb', 'middle');\n    o += t(84, 74, 'al-Kindi, al-Farabi,', '', 'middle') + t(84, 88, 'Ibn Sina, Ibn Rushd', '', 'middle');\n    o += t(84, 108, 'e.g. world is eternal', 'lb', 'middle');\n    o += rect(166, 8, 140, 112, 'box2', 8) + t(236, 26, 'Kalam', 'k2', 'middle');\n    o += t(236, 42, 'starts from revelation;', 'lb', 'middle') + t(236, 54, 'reason defends doctrine', 'lb', 'middle');\n    o += t(236, 74, 'Mu‘tazila: reason,', '', 'middle') + t(236, 88, 'free will; Ash‘aris', '', 'middle');\n    o += t(236, 108, 'e.g. world created in time', 'lb', 'middle');\n    o += rect(14, 140, 292, 30, 'box', 8) + t(160, 159, 'al-Ghazali, Incoherence of the Philosophers', 'b', 'middle');\n    o += arrow(84, 140, 84, 123) + t(92, 135, 'critique', 'lb', 'start');\n    o += rect(14, 192, 292, 30, 'box1', 8) + t(160, 211, 'Ibn Rushd, Incoherence of the Incoherence', 'k1', 'middle');\n    o += arrow(236, 192, 236, 173) + t(244, 186, 'reply', 'lb', 'start');\n    return { svg: svg(o, 228), cap: 'Two rational traditions. Al-Ghazali, himself an Ash‘ari theologian, attacked the philosophers; Ibn Rushd replied about a century later.' };\n  };\n\n  // ------------------------------------------------------- waqf\n  G.ihum242Waqf = function () {\n    var o = '';\n    o += rect(14, 8, 120, 40, 'box', 8) + t(74, 25, 'Founder (waqif)', 'b', 'middle') + t(74, 39, 'gives up ownership', 'lb', 'middle');\n    o += arrow(134, 28, 182, 28);\n    o += rect(184, 8, 122, 40, 'box1', 8) + t(245, 25, 'Property', 'k1', 'middle') + t(245, 39, 'shops, land, baths', 'lb', 'middle');\n    o += arrow(245, 48, 245, 70);\n    o += t(253, 63, 'rent', 'lb', 'start');\n    o += rect(184, 72, 122, 40, 'box1', 8) + t(245, 89, 'Income', 'k1', 'middle') + t(245, 103, 'in perpetuity', 'lb', 'middle');\n    o += arrow(245, 112, 245, 134);\n    o += rect(14, 136, 292, 52, 'box3', 8) + t(160, 154, 'Beneficiaries named in the deed', 'k3', 'middle');\n    o += t(160, 170, 'madrasa · mosque · hospital · fountain', '', 'middle') + t(160, 182, 'or the founder’s family (family waqf)', 'lb', 'middle');\n    o += rect(14, 72, 140, 40, 'box2', 8) + t(84, 89, 'Administrator', 'k2', 'middle') + t(84, 103, '(mutawalli); qadi oversees', 'lb', 'middle');\n    o += arrow(154, 92, 182, 92);\n    return { svg: svg(o, 194), cap: 'A waqf locks property out of the market for good and dedicates its income to the purpose the founder names.' };\n  };\n\n  // ------------------------------------------------------- millet\n  G.ihum242Millet = function () {\n    var o = rect(90, 8, 140, 32, 'box', 10) + t(160, 22, 'Sultan', 'b', 'middle') + t(160, 34, 'Ottoman state and courts', 'lb', 'middle');\n    var M = [['Greek Orthodox', 'Patriarch of', 'Constantinople'], ['Armenian', 'Armenian', 'Patriarch'], ['Jewish', 'Chief Rabbi', '(Hahambaşı)']];\n    M.forEach(function (m, k) {\n      var x = 14 + k * 100;\n      o += curve([[160, 40], [160, 52], [x + 46, 52], [x + 46, 64]], 'c4', true);\n      o += rect(x, 66, 92, 56, 'box1', 8) + t(x + 46, 84, m[0], 'k1', 'middle') + t(x + 46, 100, m[1], 'lb', 'middle') + t(x + 46, 113, m[2], 'lb', 'middle');\n    });\n    o += rect(14, 134, 292, 48, 'box2', 8);\n    o += t(160, 152, 'Inside each community: its own leaders', 'k2', 'middle');\n    o += t(160, 168, 'marriage, divorce, inheritance, schools, taxes', 'lb', 'middle');\n    return { svg: svg(o, 188), cap: 'The communities as formalized in the 19th century. Earlier arrangements were more local and varied than a single \"system\".' };\n  };\n\n  // ------------------------------------------------------- mosque plans\n  G.ihum242MosquePlan = function (a) {\n    a = a || {};\n    var o = '';\n    if (a.type === 'iwan') {\n      o += t(14, 12, 'Mecca ↑', 'k2', 'start');\n      o += rect(60, 20, 200, 170, 'box', 4);\n      o += ln(60, 20, 260, 20, 'c2') + t(160, 15, 'qibla wall', 'k2', 'middle');\n      o += rect(110, 78, 100, 62, 'fill1', 2) + t(160, 113, 'courtyard', 'it', 'middle');\n      o += rect(122, 24, 76, 26, 'box1', 3) + t(160, 41, 'dome chamber', 'lb', 'middle');\n      o += rect(136, 50, 48, 28, 'box1', 3) + t(160, 68, 'iwan', 'k1', 'middle');\n      o += rect(136, 140, 48, 28, 'box1', 3) + t(160, 158, 'iwan', 'k1', 'middle');\n      o += rect(70, 94, 40, 30, 'box1', 3) + t(90, 113, 'iwan', 'k1', 'middle');\n      o += rect(210, 94, 40, 30, 'box1', 3) + t(230, 113, 'iwan', 'k1', 'middle');\n      [[66, 26], [66, 148], [214, 26], [214, 148]].forEach(function (p) { o += rect(p[0], p[1], 40, 34, 'box', 2); });\n      o += t(86, 47, 'rooms', 'lb', 'middle') + t(234, 47, 'rooms', 'lb', 'middle') + t(86, 169, 'rooms', 'lb', 'middle') + t(234, 169, 'rooms', 'lb', 'middle');\n      o += curve([[184, 64], [272, 64]], 'c4') + t(276, 61, 'qibla', 'b', 'start') + t(276, 73, 'iwan', 'b', 'start');\n      o += t(160, 204, 'an iwan on each axis of the courtyard', 'lb', 'middle');\n      return { svg: svg(o, 210), cap: 'The four-iwan plan, used for Iranian mosques (Friday Mosque of Isfahan) and for madrasas, with teaching rooms around the court.' };\n    }\n    o += t(14, 14, 'Mecca ↑', 'k2', 'start') + t(114, 14, 'qibla wall', 'k2', 'middle');\n    o += rect(24, 22, 180, 160, 'box', 3);\n    o += ln(24, 22, 204, 22, 'c2');\n    o += '<path d=\"M106 22 A8 8 0 0 0 122 22\" class=\"c2\"/>';\n    o += rect(127, 24, 9, 10, 'solid2', 1);\n    for (var r = 0; r < 3; r++) for (var c = 0; c < 9; c++) o += '<circle cx=\"' + (36 + c * 19.5) + '\" cy=\"' + (52 + r * 13) + '\" r=\"1.8\" class=\"solid4\"/>';\n    o += t(114, 100, 'prayer hall (columns)', 'lb', 'middle');\n    o += ln(24, 106, 204, 106, 'c4 dash');\n    o += rect(50, 112, 128, 64, 'fill1', 2) + t(114, 134, 'courtyard (sahn)', 'it', 'middle');\n    o += '<circle cx=\"114\" cy=\"154\" r=\"5\" class=\"box1\"/>';\n    o += rect(104, 182, 20, 16, 'solid1', 2);\n    o += curve([[114, 30], [114, 40], [212, 40]], 'c4') + t(216, 44, 'mihrab', 'b', 'start');\n    o += curve([[136, 29], [212, 29]], 'c4') + t(216, 32, 'minbar', 'b', 'start');\n    o += curve([[119, 154], [212, 154]], 'c4') + t(216, 152, 'ablution', 'b', 'start') + t(216, 164, 'fountain', 'b', 'start');\n    o += curve([[124, 190], [212, 190]], 'c4') + t(216, 194, 'minaret', 'b', 'start');\n    o += t(216, 72, 'rows of', 'lb', 'start') + t(216, 84, 'worshippers', 'lb', 'start') + t(216, 96, 'face the wall', 'lb', 'start');\n    return { svg: svg(o, 202), cap: 'A hypostyle (\"many columns\") congregational mosque, schematic: the long qibla wall faces Mecca, so worshippers stand in wide rows.' };\n  };\n\n  // ------------------------------------------------------- muqarnas\n  G.ihum242Muqarnas = function () {\n    var o = '';\n    function niche(x, y, w, h, cls) {\n      var m = x + w / 2;\n      return '<path d=\"M' + x + ' ' + (y + h) + ' L' + x + ' ' + (y + h * 0.45) + ' Q' + x + ' ' + y + ' ' + m + ' ' + y +\n        ' Q' + (x + w) + ' ' + y + ' ' + (x + w) + ' ' + (y + h * 0.45) + ' L' + (x + w) + ' ' + (y + h) + ' Z\" class=\"' + cls + '\"/>';\n    }\n    // Tiers widen as they rise: each one corbels out over the one below.\n    var tiers = [[3, 124], [4, 96], [5, 68], [6, 40]], w = 26, h = 26;\n    tiers.forEach(function (tr, k) {\n      var n = tr[0], y = tr[1], x0 = 120 - n * w / 2;\n      for (var i = 0; i < n; i++) o += niche(x0 + i * w, y, w, h, k % 2 ? 'box1' : 'box');\n    });\n    o += rect(30, 12, 180, 24, 'fill1', 2) + t(120, 28, 'dome or ceiling', 'k1', 'middle');\n    o += rect(66, 152, 108, 14, 'solid4', 2) + t(120, 180, 'wall corner or arch below', 'lb', 'middle');\n    o += curve([[218, 150], [218, 46]], 'c4', true);\n    o += t(226, 78, 'tiers of', 'k2', 'start') + t(226, 91, 'small niches', 'k2', 'start') + t(226, 104, 'step outward', 'k2', 'start') + t(226, 117, 'as they rise', 'k2', 'start');\n    return { svg: svg(o, 186), cap: 'Muqarnas, in elevation (schematic): rows of small niches, each tier projecting over the last, bridge a square or arch to a curved dome.' };\n  };\n\n  // ------------------------------------------------------- qasida\n  G.ihum242Qasida = function () {\n    var P = [['Nasib', 'prelude:', 'deserted camp'], ['Rahil', 'journey:', 'camel, desert'], ['Gharad', 'purpose:', 'praise, boast']];\n    var o = '';\n    P.forEach(function (p, k) {\n      var x = 14 + k * 104;\n      o += rect(x, 14, 88, 64, k === 2 ? 'box1' : 'box', 8) + t(x + 44, 34, p[0], k === 2 ? 'k1' : 'b', 'middle');\n      o += t(x + 44, 52, p[1], 'lb', 'middle') + t(x + 44, 66, p[2], 'lb', 'middle');\n      if (k < 2) o += arrow(x + 89, 46, x + 103, 46);\n    });\n    o += t(14, 104, 'Every line (bayt): two half-lines, one meter, one rhyme', 'b', 'start');\n    for (var k = 0; k < 4; k++) {\n      var y = 116 + k * 16;\n      o += rect(14, y, 128, 8, 'solid4', 2) + rect(150, y, 128, 8, 'solid4', 2) + rect(280, y - 2, 24, 12, 'box2', 3) + t(292, y + 8, '-li', 'k2', 'middle');\n    }\n    o += t(14, 194, 'monorhyme: the same rhyme closes every line of the poem', 'lb', 'start');\n    return { svg: svg(o, 200), cap: 'The classical tripartite ode, as later critics described it (Ibn Qutayba, 9th c.); many qasidas vary or drop parts.' };\n  };\n\n  // ------------------------------------------------------- ghazal\n  G.ihum242Ghazal = function () {\n    var o = t(14, 14, 'Ghazal: couplets (bayts), each a closed thought', 'b', 'start');\n    var rows = [['1', true, true, ''], ['2', false, true, ''], ['3', false, true, ''], ['…', false, true, ''], ['last', false, true, 'pen name (takhallus)']];\n    rows.forEach(function (r, k) {\n      var y = 28 + k * 30;\n      o += t(30, y + 13, r[0], 'lb', 'end');\n      o += rect(36, y + 4, 110, 10, 'solid4', 2) + (r[1] ? rect(122, y + 1, 24, 16, 'box2', 3) : '');\n      o += rect(152, y + 4, 110, 10, 'solid4', 2) + rect(238, y + 1, 24, 16, 'box2', 3);\n      if (r[3]) o += t(36, y + 28, r[3], 'k1', 'start');\n    });\n    o += t(270, 46, 'rhyme +', 'k2', 'start') + t(270, 58, 'refrain', 'k2', 'start') + t(270, 70, '(radif)', 'lb', 'start');\n    o += t(14, 196, 'Persian ghazals typically run about 5–15 couplets.', 'lb', 'start');\n    return { svg: svg(o, 202), cap: 'The opening couplet rhymes in both lines; then only the second line of each couplet carries the rhyme and refrain. The poet signs the last.' };\n  };\n\n  // ------------------------------------------------------- modern era\n  var MOD = [[1798, 'Napoleon invades Egypt'], [1839, 'Tanzimat begins (Gülhane edict)'], [1876, 'Ottoman constitution'],\n    [1916, 'Sykes–Picot agreement'], [1924, 'Ottoman caliphate abolished'], [1947, 'Partition of India; Pakistan'],\n    [1978, 'Said, Orientalism'], [1979, 'Iranian Revolution']];\n  G.ihum242ModernMod = MOD;\n  G.ihum242Modern = function (a) {\n    a = a || {};\n    var o = ln(64, 10, 64, 10 + MOD.length * 23 - 8, 'c4');\n    MOD.forEach(function (m, k) {\n      var y = 14 + k * 23, on = has(a.hi, m[0]);\n      o += '<circle cx=\"64\" cy=\"' + y + '\" r=\"' + (on ? 5 : 3.2) + '\" class=\"' + (on ? 'solid1' : 'solid4') + '\"/>';\n      o += t(52, y + 4, String(m[0]), on ? 'k1' : 'b', 'end');\n      o += t(76, y + 4, m[1], on ? 'k1' : '', 'start');\n    });\n    return { svg: svg(o, MOD.length * 23 + 6), cap: a.cap || 'Landmarks of the modern era, from the first colonial shock to the postcolonial critique.' };\n  };\n\n  // ------------------------------------------------------- population\n  G.ihum242Population = function () {\n    var sx = scale(0, 100, 92, 300);\n    var o = t(14, 14, 'Muslims as a share of world population', 'b', 'start');\n    [['2010', 23.9], ['2020', 25.6]].forEach(function (r, k) {\n      var y = 24 + k * 30;\n      o += rect(sx(0), y, sx(100) - sx(0), 20, 'box', 4) + rect(sx(0), y, sx(r[1]) - sx(0), 20, 'solid1', 4);\n      o += t(84, y + 14, r[0], 'b', 'end') + t(sx(r[1]) + 5, y + 14, r[1] + '%', 'k1', 'start');\n    });\n    o += t(14, 106, 'Within Islam: Sunni and Shi‘i (approx.)', 'b', 'start');\n    var y = 116;\n    o += rect(sx(0), y, sx(87) - sx(0), 22, 'solid1', 4) + rect(sx(87), y, sx(90) - sx(87), 22, 'fill1', 0) + rect(sx(90), y, sx(100) - sx(90), 22, 'solid2', 4);\n    o += t(84, y + 15, 'all Muslims', 'b', 'end');\n    o += t(sx(40), y + 15, 'Sunni 87–90%', 'w', 'middle');\n    o += t(300, y + 38, 'Shi‘i 10–13%', 'k2', 'end');\n    o += t(14, 178, '≈ 2.0 billion Muslims in 2020 (+347 million since 2010)', 'lb', 'start');\n    return { svg: svg(o, 186), cap: 'Pew Research Center estimates (2025 for 2010–2020 shares; 2009 for the Sunni–Shi‘i range, which is approximate).' };\n  };\n})();\n";
// The review page itself, shared with the website build (tools/page/).
const PAGE = {"css":"  :root { --ink:#2B2118; --cream:#FDF8F2; --wk:#1F4E8C; --wk-soft:rgba(31,78,140,.10); }\n  * { box-sizing:border-box; -webkit-tap-highlight-color:transparent; }\n  html, body { margin:0; height:100%; overflow:hidden;\n    background:var(--cream); color:var(--ink);\n    font-family:-apple-system,system-ui,sans-serif; user-select:none; -webkit-user-select:none; }\n  #app { height:100%; display:flex; flex-direction:column; }\n  header { display:flex; align-items:center; gap:10px; padding:12px 16px 2px; }\n  #quitline { text-align:center; padding-bottom:2px; }\n  #bar { flex:1; height:5px; border-radius:3px; background:rgba(0,0,0,.09); overflow:hidden; }\n  #fill { height:100%; width:0%; background:var(--wk); transition:width .25s ease, background .25s; }\n  #count { font-size:12px; opacity:.55; font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums; }\n  #quit { font-size:11px; opacity:.45; }\n  #logo { display:flex; align-items:center; gap:6px; padding:4px 6px 4px 2px;\n          font-size:13px; font-weight:800; letter-spacing:.06em; color:var(--wk); transition:color .25s; }\n  #logo svg { width:19px; height:19px; }\n  main { flex:1; position:relative; display:flex; min-height:0; }\n  /* margin:auto centres the card while it is short but still lets it scroll\n     once the answer is taller than the screen. */\n  #scroll { flex:1; display:flex; overflow-y:auto; -webkit-overflow-scrolling:touch;\n            padding:14px 20px 18px; }\n  #card { margin:auto; width:100%; max-width:560px; text-align:center; }\n\n  .chips { display:flex; justify-content:center; gap:6px; flex-wrap:wrap; }\n  .chip { font-size:10.5px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.08em; text-transform:uppercase;\n          padding:4px 9px; border-radius:20px; background:var(--wk-soft); color:var(--wk); }\n  .chip.star { background:rgba(194,98,42,.13); color:#C2622A; }\n  .term { font-family:ui-serif,\"New York\",Georgia,serif; font-weight:700;\n          font-size:36px; line-height:1.18; margin-top:18px; letter-spacing:-.01em; }\n  .weekname { font-size:13px; opacity:.5; margin-top:12px; }\n  .term.sm { font-size:21px; margin-top:12px; color:var(--wk); }\n  .simple { font-size:20px; line-height:1.42; margin-top:10px; }\n  .fx { margin:14px auto 0; padding:12px 14px; border-radius:14px; background:var(--wk-soft);\n        font-family:ui-serif,\"New York\",Georgia,serif; font-size:19px; line-height:1.6;\n        display:inline-block; max-width:100%; }\n  .fx i, .simple i, .detail i, .peek i { font-family:ui-serif,\"New York\",Georgia,serif; }\n\n  /* hand-built maths: fractions, sums, hats */\n  .fr { display:inline-flex; flex-direction:column; vertical-align:middle; text-align:center;\n        margin:0 2px; line-height:1.15; }\n  .fr > span:first-child { border-bottom:1.3px solid currentColor; padding:0 3px 1px; }\n  .fr > span:last-child { padding:1px 3px 0; }\n  .fx .fr { font-size:.92em; }\n  .op { margin:0 .22em; }\n  sub, sup { font-size:.68em; line-height:0; }\n\n  .detail { margin-top:18px; text-align:start; }\n  .sect { border-top:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.09); margin-top:13px; padding-top:10px; }\n  .sect h3 { margin:0 0 6px; font-size:10.5px; font-weight:700; opacity:.45;\n             letter-spacing:.09em; text-transform:uppercase; }\n  .sect p { margin:0; font-size:14px; line-height:1.48; opacity:.86; }\n  .sect p + p { margin-top:6px; }\n  .row { display:flex; align-items:baseline; justify-content:space-between;\n         gap:14px; padding:4px 0; }\n  .row .lbl { font-size:13px; opacity:.65; line-height:1.35; }\n  .row .val { font-size:16px; font-weight:700; white-space:nowrap; flex:none;\n              color:var(--wk); font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums; }\n  .src { font-size:11px; opacity:.42; margin-top:4px; line-height:1.35; }\n  ol.steps { margin:0; padding-left:20px; font-size:14px; line-height:1.48; }\n  ol.steps li { padding:2px 0; opacity:.88; }\n  ol.steps li::marker { color:var(--wk); font-weight:700; }\n  .links { display:flex; flex-wrap:wrap; gap:6px; }\n  .link { font-size:13px; padding:6px 11px; border-radius:20px; border:1px solid var(--wk-soft);\n          background:#fff; color:var(--ink); line-height:1.2; }\n  .link b { color:var(--wk); font-weight:600; }\n  .graph { margin-top:4px; background:#fff; border-radius:14px; padding:8px 6px 4px;\n           border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.06); }\n  .graph svg { width:100%; height:auto; display:block; overflow:visible; }\n  .graph .cap { font-size:11.5px; opacity:.55; text-align:center; padding:2px 8px 6px; line-height:1.35; }\n  .tags { display:flex; flex-wrap:wrap; gap:5px; justify-content:center; margin-top:18px; }\n  .tag { font-size:10.5px; padding:2px 8px; border-radius:20px; border:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,.15); opacity:.6; }\n  .tag.pe { border-color:#E8A25E; color:#B4561B; background:#FFF3E6; opacity:1; font-weight:700; }\n\n  /* SVG theme */\n  svg .ax { stroke:var(--ink); stroke-width:1.3; fill:none; opacity:.75; }\n  svg .grid { stroke:var(--ink); stroke-width:.6; opacity:.12; }\n  svg .c1 { stroke:var(--wk); stroke-width:2.6; fill:none; stroke-linecap:round; }\n  svg .c2 { stroke:#C2622A; stroke-width:2.6; fill:none; stroke-linecap:round; }\n  svg .c3 { stroke:#2E7D52; stroke-width:2.4; fill:none; stroke-linecap:round; }\n  svg .c4 { stroke:var(--ink); stroke-width:1.8; fill:none; opacity:.55; stroke-linecap:round; }\n  svg .dash { stroke-dasharray:4 4; }\n  svg .faint { opacity:.4; }\n  svg .dot { fill:var(--ink); }\n  svg .fill1 { fill:var(--wk); opacity:.16; }\n  svg .fill2 { fill:#C2622A; opacity:.16; }\n  svg .fill3 { fill:#2E7D52; opacity:.16; }\n  svg .solid1 { fill:var(--wk); }\n  svg .solid2 { fill:#C2622A; }\n  svg .solid3 { fill:#2E7D52; }\n  svg .solid4 { fill:var(--ink); opacity:.35; }\n  svg .box { fill:#fff; stroke:var(--ink); stroke-width:1.2; }\n  svg .box1 { fill:var(--wk-soft); stroke:var(--wk); stroke-width:1.4; }\n  svg .box2 { fill:rgba(194,98,42,.10); stroke:#C2622A; stroke-width:1.4; }\n  svg .box3 { fill:rgba(46,125,82,.10); stroke:#2E7D52; stroke-width:1.4; }\n  svg text { font-family:-apple-system,system-ui,sans-serif; font-size:11px; fill:var(--ink); }\n  svg text.lb { font-size:10px; opacity:.6; }\n  svg text.k1 { fill:var(--wk); font-weight:700; }\n  svg text.k2 { fill:#C2622A; font-weight:700; }\n  svg text.k3 { fill:#2E7D52; font-weight:700; }\n  svg text.it { font-family:ui-serif,\"New York\",Georgia,serif; font-style:italic; font-size:12.5px; }\n  svg text.b { font-weight:700; }\n  svg text.w { fill:#fff; font-weight:700; }\n\n  #tip { flex:none; text-align:center; font-size:12px; opacity:.45; padding:0 16px 8px; }\n  #flash { position:absolute; inset:0; display:flex; align-items:center;\n           justify-content:center; font-size:34px; font-weight:800;\n           color:#fff; opacity:0; pointer-events:none; transition:opacity .18s; }\n  #flash div { text-align:center; }\n  #flash small { display:block; font-size:15px; font-weight:600; opacity:.9; margin-top:4px; }\n  footer { display:flex; gap:9px; padding:0 14px 26px; }\n  .btn { flex:1; padding:14px 0 12px; border-radius:15px; font-size:15.5px;\n         font-weight:700; color:#fff; text-align:center; line-height:1.15; }\n  .btn small { display:block; font-size:10.5px; font-weight:600; opacity:.8; margin-top:2px; }\n  .missed { background:#C0392B; }\n  .shaky  { background:#D9952B; }\n  .confident { background:#2E7D52; }\n\n  #peek { position:absolute; left:0; right:0; bottom:0; background:#fff; border-radius:20px 20px 0 0;\n          box-shadow:0 -10px 30px rgba(0,0,0,.14); padding:18px 22px 34px; transform:translateY(105%);\n          transition:transform .22s ease, visibility 0s linear .22s; text-align:center; max-height:70%;\n          overflow-y:auto; z-index:5; visibility:hidden; pointer-events:none; }\n  /* Hidden, it sits just below the card, over the grade buttons: it must not\n     catch their taps. */\n  #peek.on { transform:none; visibility:visible; pointer-events:auto; transition:transform .22s ease; }\n  #peek .grab { width:38px; height:5px; border-radius:3px; background:rgba(0,0,0,.15); margin:-6px auto 12px; }\n  #peek .term { font-size:22px; margin-top:8px; }\n  #peek .simple { font-size:16px; }\n  #peek .fx { font-size:16px; }\n  #peek .close { margin-top:16px; font-size:13px; opacity:.5; }\n\n  #done { display:none; flex-direction:column; align-items:center;\n          justify-content:center; height:100%; gap:12px; padding:0 30px; text-align:center; }\n  #done h2 { font-family:ui-serif,\"New York\",Georgia,serif; font-size:30px; margin:0; }\n  #done p { opacity:.6; margin:0; font-size:15px; line-height:1.45; }\n  #done .tally { display:flex; gap:10px; margin:6px 0; }\n  #done .tally div { padding:10px 14px; border-radius:14px; color:#fff; font-weight:700; font-size:20px; min-width:74px; }\n  #done .tally small { display:block; font-size:10.5px; font-weight:600; opacity:.85; }\n","body":"<div id=\"app\">\n  <header>\n    <span id=\"logo\">\n      <svg viewBox=\"0 0 40 40\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\">\n        <path d=\"M6 5v29h29\" stroke-width=\"2.6\"/>\n        <path d=\"M10 29c6-1 9-5 12-11s6-9 11-10\" stroke-width=\"2.6\"/>\n        <path d=\"M10 12l23 17\" stroke-width=\"2\" opacity=\".55\"/>\n      </svg>IHUM 242\n    </span>\n    <div id=\"bar\"><div id=\"fill\"></div></div>\n    <span id=\"count\"></span>\n  </header>\n  <div id=\"quitline\"><span id=\"quit\">__QUIT__</span></div>\n  <main>\n    <div id=\"scroll\"><div id=\"card\"></div></div>\n    <div id=\"flash\"></div>\n    <div id=\"peek\"></div>\n  </main>\n  <div id=\"tip\">Tap to flip</div>\n  <footer>\n    <div class=\"btn missed\" data-act=\"missed\">Missed<small>again now</small></div>\n    <div class=\"btn shaky\" data-act=\"shaky\">Shaky<small>in 1 hour</small></div>\n    <div class=\"btn confident\" data-act=\"confident\">Confident<small id=\"confHint\">later</small></div>\n  </footer>\n</div>\n\n<div id=\"done\">\n  <h2>Done</h2>\n  <p id=\"doneText\"></p>\n  <div class=\"tally\">\n    <div class=\"missed\"><span id=\"tM\">0</span><small>missed</small></div>\n    <div class=\"shaky\"><span id=\"tS\">0</span><small>shaky</small></div>\n    <div class=\"confident\"><span id=\"tC\">0</span><small>confident</small></div>\n  </div>\n  <p id=\"laterText\"></p>\n  __DONE_ACTIONS__\n</div>\n","js":"// The review page shared by the phone (Scriptable) and the website. Each\n// host fills these in: the phone injects one session's cards when it builds\n// the page; the website starts with none and calls startSession() from its\n// menu.\nlet CARDS = /*__CARDS__*/[];\nconst WEEKS = /*__WEEKS__*/[];\nlet PRACTICE = /*__PRACTICE__*/false;\n// Page text and behaviour that differ between a touch screen and a keyboard.\nconst UI = Object.assign({\n  tipFront: 'Tap to flip',\n  tipBack: 'Left = Missed  \\u00b7  Right = Confident',\n  thirds: true,\n}, /*__UI__*/{});\nconst LADDER = [4, 8, 24];\nlet results = [];\nlet later = [];              // shaky cards waiting out their hour: {card, at}\nlet i = 0, flipped = false;\n// Where each card sits on the ladder, so the Confident button can say how\n// long it will be gone. Kept in step with the scheduler.\nlet steps = {};\nlet wakeTimer = null;\n\n/** Starts a fresh session on these cards. */\nfunction startSession(cards, practice) {\n  CARDS = cards.slice();\n  PRACTICE = !!practice;\n  results = [];\n  later = [];\n  i = 0;\n  steps = {};\n  CARDS.forEach(function (c) { steps[c.id] = c.step || 0; });\n  clearTimeout(wakeTimer);\n  document.querySelector('#done').style.display = 'none';\n  document.querySelector('#app').style.display = 'flex';\n  render();\n}\n\nconst $ = function (s) { return document.querySelector(s); };\n\n/** A shaky card whose hour is up goes back in at the front of the queue. */\nfunction releaseLater() {\n  const now = Date.now();\n  for (let k = later.length - 1; k >= 0; k--) {\n    if (later[k].at <= now) {\n      CARDS.splice(i, 0, later[k].card);\n      later.splice(k, 1);\n    }\n  }\n}\n\n/** \"Week 3\" for ECON 381; the unit's own name for classes organised by units. */\nfunction unitLabel(w) {\n  return UI.unitTitles && WEEKS[w - 1] ? WEEKS[w - 1].title : 'Week ' + w;\n}\n\nfunction setWeek(c) {\n  const w = WEEKS[c.week - 1];\n  const r = document.documentElement.style;\n  r.setProperty('--wk', w.color);\n  r.setProperty('--wk-soft', w.soft);\n}\n\nfunction chips(c) {\n  const w = WEEKS[c.week - 1];\n  let h = '<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">' + unitLabel(c.week) + '</span>';\n  if (c.starred) h += '<span class=\"chip star\">★ Tested</span>';\n  return h + '</div>';\n}\n\nfunction render() {\n  releaseLater();\n  const c = CARDS[i];\n  if (!c) return finishScreen();\n  flipped = false;\n  setWeek(c);\n  hidePeek();\n  $('#tip').textContent = UI.tipFront;\n  const doneCount = results.length;\n  $('#count').textContent = Math.min(i + 1, CARDS.length) + '/' + CARDS.length;\n  $('#fill').style.width = ((i / Math.max(CARDS.length, 1)) * 100) + '%';\n  $('#card').innerHTML = chips(c) +\n    '<div class=\"term\">' + c.term + '</div>' +\n    (UI.unitTitles ? '' : '<div class=\"weekname\">' + WEEKS[c.week - 1].title + '</div>');\n  $('#scroll').scrollTop = 0;\n  $('footer').style.visibility = 'hidden';\n}\n\nfunction flip() {\n  const c = CARDS[i];\n  if (!c) return;\n  flipped = !flipped;\n  if (!flipped) return render();\n  let h = chips(c) + '<div class=\"term sm\">' + c.term + '</div>';\n  h += '<div class=\"simple\">' + c.simple + '</div>';\n  if (c.formula) h += '<div class=\"fx\">' + c.formula + '</div>';\n  h += detail(c);\n  if (c.tags && c.tags.length) {\n    h += '<div class=\"tags\">' + c.tags.map(function (t) {\n      return '<span class=\"tag' + (/^PE/.test(t) ? ' pe' : '') + '\">' + esc(t) + '</span>';\n    }).join('') + '</div>';\n  }\n  $('#card').innerHTML = h;\n  $('#scroll').scrollTop = 0;\n  $('#tip').textContent = UI.tipBack;\n  const step = Math.min(steps[c.id] || 0, LADDER.length - 1);\n  $('#confHint').textContent = 'in ' + (LADDER[step] === 24 ? '1 day' : LADDER[step] + ' hours');\n  $('footer').style.visibility = 'visible';\n}\n\n/* ----------------------------------------------------------- answer detail */\n\nfunction section(title, body) {\n  if (!body) return '';\n  return '<div class=\"sect\"><h3>' + title + '</h3>' + body + '</div>';\n}\n\nfunction paras(x) {\n  if (!x) return '';\n  return (Array.isArray(x) ? x : [x]).map(function (p) { return '<p>' + p + '</p>'; }).join('');\n}\n\n/** The answer's small print, in a fixed order. A section with nothing to show\n *  is left out rather than shown empty. */\nfunction detail(c) {\n  let d = '';\n  d += section('Additional info', paras(c.more));\n  if (c.links && c.links.length) {\n    d += section('Connections', '<div class=\"links\">' + c.links.map(function (l) {\n      return '<span class=\"link\" data-peek=\"' + l.id + '\"><b>' + l.term + '</b></span>';\n    }).join('') + '</div>' + (c.connectNote ? '<p style=\"margin-top:8px\">' + c.connectNote + '</p>' : ''));\n  }\n  d += section('Real-world example', paras(c.example));\n  if (c.numbers && c.numbers.length) {\n    let rows = c.numbers.map(function (n) {\n      return '<div class=\"row\"><span class=\"lbl\">' + n[0] + '</span><span class=\"val\">' + n[1] + '</span></div>';\n    }).join('');\n    if (c.numbersSource) rows += '<div class=\"src\">' + c.numbersSource + '</div>';\n    d += section('By the numbers', rows);\n  }\n  if (c.played && c.played.length) {\n    d += section('Played out', '<ol class=\"steps\">' + c.played.map(function (s) {\n      return '<li>' + s + '</li>';\n    }).join('') + '</ol>');\n  }\n  if (c.graph) {\n    const g = drawGraph(c.graph);\n    if (g) d += section('Graph', '<div class=\"graph\">' + g.svg +\n      (g.cap ? '<div class=\"cap\">' + g.cap + '</div>' : '') + '</div>');\n  }\n  return d ? '<div class=\"detail\">' + d + '</div>' : '';\n}\n\nfunction drawGraph(spec) {\n  try {\n    const key = typeof spec === 'string' ? spec : spec.key;\n    const fn = window.GRAPHS && window.GRAPHS[key];\n    return fn ? fn(spec.args || {}) : null;\n  } catch (e) {\n    return null;\n  }\n}\n\n/* -------------------------------------------------------- connection peek */\n\nfunction showPeek(id) {\n  const c = CARDS[i];\n  const l = (c.links || []).filter(function (x) { return x.id === id; })[0];\n  if (!l) return;\n  const w = WEEKS[l.week - 1];\n  let h = '<div class=\"grab\"></div><div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\" style=\"background:' + w.soft +\n    ';color:' + w.color + '\">' + unitLabel(l.week) + '</span></div>' +\n    '<div class=\"term\" style=\"color:' + w.color + '\">' + l.term + '</div>' +\n    '<div class=\"simple\">' + l.simple + '</div>';\n  if (l.formula) h += '<div class=\"fx\">' + l.formula + '</div>';\n  h += '<div class=\"close\">Tap to close</div>';\n  $('#peek').innerHTML = h;\n  $('#peek').classList.add('on');\n}\n\nfunction hidePeek() { $('#peek').classList.remove('on'); }\nfunction peekOpen() { return $('#peek').classList.contains('on'); }\n\n/* ----------------------------------------------------------------- grading */\n\nfunction grade(rating) {\n  if (!flipped || !CARDS[i]) return;\n  const card = CARDS[i];\n  const t = Date.now();\n  // The next card isn't on screen for another moment; a second press in\n  // that gap must not grade it unseen.\n  flipped = false;\n  results.push({ id: card.id, rating: rating, t: t });\n  // Handed to Scriptable straight away so it is saved while the session is\n  // still open, not only on the way out. The sequence number is what the\n  // native side dedupes on: a card that comes round again is graded again.\n  ping('grade?id=' + encodeURIComponent(card.id) + '&rating=' + rating +\n       '&seq=' + (results.length - 1) + '&t=' + t);\n  const colors = { missed: '#C0392B', shaky: '#D9952B', confident: '#2E7D52' };\n  flash(rating.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + rating.slice(1), comesBack(card, rating), colors[rating]);\n\n  if (rating === 'missed') {\n    // Didn't know it: to the back of the deck, so it comes round again this\n    // session instead of disappearing.\n    steps[card.id] = 0;\n    CARDS.splice(i, 1);\n    CARDS.push(card);\n  } else if (rating === 'shaky') {\n    steps[card.id] = Math.max((steps[card.id] || 0) - 1, 0);\n    CARDS.splice(i, 1);\n    // In cram mode nothing is scheduled, so a shaky card simply goes back a\n    // few places; otherwise it waits out its hour.\n    if (PRACTICE) CARDS.splice(Math.min(i + 5, CARDS.length), 0, card);\n    else later.push({ card: card, at: t + 60 * 60 * 1000 });\n  } else {\n    steps[card.id] = Math.min((steps[card.id] || 0) + 1, LADDER.length - 1);\n    i++;\n  }\n  setTimeout(render, 170);\n}\n\n/** Fires an econ381:// request that the native side intercepts and blocks.\n *  Sent from a hidden iframe so the page itself never navigates. */\nfunction ping(path) {\n  if (window.ECON_HOST) { window.ECON_HOST.ping(path); return; }\n  const f = document.createElement('iframe');\n  f.style.display = 'none';\n  f.src = 'econ381://' + path;\n  document.body.appendChild(f);\n  setTimeout(function () { f.remove(); }, 80);\n}\n\n/** When a graded card will be seen again, said the moment it's graded, so\n *  the schedule is visible rather than taken on trust. Read before the\n *  card's step moves. */\nfunction comesBack(card, rating) {\n  if (PRACTICE) return rating === 'missed' ? 'again at the end' : rating === 'shaky' ? 'again in a few cards' : 'cram \\u00b7 not scheduled';\n  if (rating === 'missed') return 'again at the end of this session';\n  if (rating === 'shaky') return 'back in 1 hour';\n  const h = LADDER[Math.min(steps[card.id] || 0, LADDER.length - 1)];\n  return 'back in ' + (h === 24 ? '1 day' : h + ' hours');\n}\n\nlet flashTimer = null;\nfunction flash(text, sub, color) {\n  const f = $('#flash');\n  f.innerHTML = '<div>' + esc(text) + '<small>' + esc(sub) + '</small></div>';\n  f.style.background = color;\n  f.style.opacity = '.94';\n  clearTimeout(flashTimer);\n  flashTimer = setTimeout(function () { f.style.opacity = '0'; }, 520);\n}\n\nfunction finishScreen() {\n  $('#app').style.display = 'none';\n  $('#done').style.display = 'flex';\n  const last = {};\n  results.forEach(function (r) { last[r.id] = r.rating; });\n  const n = { missed: 0, shaky: 0, confident: 0 };\n  results.forEach(function (r) { n[r.rating]++; });\n  const seen = Object.keys(last).length;\n  $('#doneText').textContent = seen + ' card' + (seen === 1 ? '' : 's') + ' · ' +\n    results.length + ' answer' + (results.length === 1 ? '' : 's');\n  $('#tM').textContent = n.missed;\n  $('#tS').textContent = n.shaky;\n  $('#tC').textContent = n.confident;\n  if (later.length) {\n    const at = Math.min.apply(null, later.map(function (x) { return x.at; }));\n    const d = new Date(at);\n    let h = d.getHours(); const m = String(d.getMinutes()).padStart(2, '0');\n    const ap = h >= 12 ? 'pm' : 'am'; h = h % 12 || 12;\n    $('#laterText').textContent = later.length + ' shaky card' + (later.length === 1 ? '' : 's') +\n      ' come' + (later.length === 1 ? 's' : '') + ' back at ' + h + ':' + m + ap +\n      '. Leave this open and ' + (later.length === 1 ? 'it' : 'they') + '’ll reappear, or run the script again then.';\n    // Wait for them here as well: if the screen is left open, they appear.\n    wakeTimer = setTimeout(function wake() {\n      if (later.some(function (x) { return x.at <= Date.now(); })) {\n        $('#done').style.display = 'none';\n        $('#app').style.display = 'flex';\n        render();\n      } else wakeTimer = setTimeout(wake, 30000);\n    }, 30000);\n  } else {\n    $('#laterText').textContent = '';\n  }\n}\n\nfunction esc(s) {\n  return String(s == null ? '' : s).replace(/[&<>\"]/g, function (ch) {\n    return { '&': '&amp;', '<': '&lt;', '>': '&gt;', '\"': '&quot;' }[ch];\n  });\n}\n\ndocument.addEventListener('click', function (e) {\n  if (peekOpen()) { hidePeek(); return; }\n  const pk = e.target.closest('[data-peek]');\n  if (pk) { showPeek(pk.dataset.peek); return; }\n  const act = e.target.closest('[data-act]');\n  if (!act) return;\n  if (!flipped) { flip(); return; }   // the buttons only grade after the reveal\n  grade(act.dataset.act);\n});\n\n/* The thirds are worked out from where the tap landed rather than from an\n   overlay, so the answer can scroll normally underneath. */\nconst scroll = $('#scroll');\nlet dragged = false;\nscroll.addEventListener('touchstart', function () { dragged = false; }, { passive: true });\nscroll.addEventListener('touchmove', function () { dragged = true; }, { passive: true });\nscroll.addEventListener('click', function (e) {\n  if (dragged) { dragged = false; return; }       // a tap that ended a scroll is not a grade\n  if (e.target.closest('[data-act],[data-peek]')) return;\n  if (peekOpen()) return;\n  if (!flipped || !UI.thirds) { flip(); return; }\n  const x = e.clientX / scroll.clientWidth;\n  if (x < 0.30) grade('missed');\n  else if (x > 0.70) grade('confident');\n  else flip();\n});\n\nif (CARDS.length) startSession(CARDS, PRACTICE);\n"};

const CARDS = DECK.cards;
const BY_ID = Object.fromEntries(CARDS.map((c) => [c.id, c]));
const WEEKS = DECK.weeks;

// --------------------------------------------------------------- storage

/** iCloud when it's on, so progress follows you between devices and
 *  survives re-pasting the script; the local folder when it isn't. */
function storage() {
  try {
    const fm = FileManager.iCloud();
    fm.documentsDirectory();
    return fm;
  } catch (e) {
    return FileManager.local();
  }
}

function progressPath(fm) {
  return fm.joinPath(fm.documentsDirectory(), PROGRESS_FILE);
}

async function readProgress(fm) {
  const p = progressPath(fm);
  try {
    if (!fm.fileExists(p)) return null;
    if (fm.isFileStoredIniCloud && fm.isFileStoredIniCloud(p) && !fm.isFileDownloaded(p)) {
      await fm.downloadFileFromiCloud(p);
    }
    const data = JSON.parse(fm.readString(p));
    return data && data.cards ? data : null;
  } catch (e) {
    return null;
  }
}

/** The iCloud copy, or the local one if a save ever had to fall back to it
 *  (whichever holds the more recent grade). */
async function loadProgress() {
  const latest = (d) => Math.max(0, ...Object.values(d.cards).map((s) => s.last || 0));
  const copies = [await readProgress(storage()), await readProgress(FileManager.local())]
    .filter(Boolean)
    .sort((a, b) => latest(b) - latest(a));
  return copies[0] || { cards: {} };
}

function saveProgress(progress) {
  const body = JSON.stringify(progress);
  try {
    const fm = storage();
    fm.writeString(progressPath(fm), body);
  } catch (e) {
    // iCloud refused the write: keep the session's grades locally rather
    // than losing them.
    const fm = FileManager.local();
    fm.writeString(progressPath(fm), body);
  }
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ sync
//
// The same progress the website keeps, in the Supabase project the Arabic
// app (Wazn) uses. The phone signs in with the Wazn login it already keeps
// in the Keychain (wazn.email / wazn.password, shared with the Wazn
// scripts), pulls the website's progress, combines it with its own
// (mergeProgress: the latest grade per card wins) and pushes the result.
// Without a login or a connection everything still works from iCloud.

const SUPABASE_URL = "https://fphpcfecgnfoogfaeihu.supabase.co";
// Publishable key, meant to ship to apps; row-level security keeps each
// person's row private.
const SUPABASE_KEY = "sb_publishable_UFHFJ-b988nrZ_QP2AbQ4g_64Jq_5s2";
const KEY_EMAIL = "wazn.email";
const KEY_PASSWORD = "wazn.password";

async function credentials(promptIfMissing) {
  if (Keychain.contains(KEY_EMAIL) && Keychain.contains(KEY_PASSWORD)) {
    return { email: Keychain.get(KEY_EMAIL), password: Keychain.get(KEY_PASSWORD) };
  }
  if (!promptIfMissing) return null;
  const a = new Alert();
  a.title = "Sign in to sync";
  a.message = "Your Arabic app (Wazn) email and password. Kept in the iOS Keychain on this phone only.";
  a.addTextField("Email", "");
  a.addSecureTextField("Password");
  a.addAction("Sign in");
  a.addCancelAction("Cancel");
  if ((await a.presentAlert()) === -1) return null;
  const email = a.textFieldValue(0).trim(), password = a.textFieldValue(1);
  if (!email || !password) return null;
  Keychain.set(KEY_EMAIL, email);
  Keychain.set(KEY_PASSWORD, password);
  return { email, password };
}

async function signIn(promptIfMissing) {
  const creds = await credentials(promptIfMissing);
  if (!creds) return null;
  const req = new Request(SUPABASE_URL + "/auth/v1/token?grant_type=password");
  req.method = "POST";
  req.headers = { apikey: SUPABASE_KEY, "Content-Type": "application/json" };
  req.body = JSON.stringify({ email: creds.email, password: creds.password });
  let res = null;
  try { res = await req.loadJSON(); } catch (e) { return null; }   // offline
  if (!res || !res.access_token) {
    // A changed password: forget it so the next sign-in asks again.
    if (promptIfMissing) { Keychain.remove(KEY_EMAIL); Keychain.remove(KEY_PASSWORD); }
    return null;
  }
  return { token: res.access_token, userId: res.user && res.user.id };
}

function authHeaders(sess) {
  return { apikey: SUPABASE_KEY, Authorization: "Bearer " + sess.token, "Content-Type": "application/json" };
}

async function pullRemote(sess) {
  const req = new Request(SUPABASE_URL + "/rest/v1/" + COURSE.table + "?select=data" +
    (COURSE.course ? "&course_id=eq." + encodeURIComponent(COURSE.course) : ""));
  req.headers = authHeaders(sess);
  const rows = await req.loadJSON();
  if (!Array.isArray(rows)) throw new Error("couldn't read progress");
  return rows[0] ? rows[0].data : null;
}

/** Pull, combine, push: whatever the website graded comes in, and
 *  whatever the phone graded goes up. Returns whether it got through. */
async function syncNow(sess, progress) {
  if (!sess) return false;
  try {
    mergeProgress(progress, await pullRemote(sess));
    saveProgress(progress);
    const req = new Request(SUPABASE_URL + "/rest/v1/" + COURSE.table + "?on_conflict=" + (COURSE.course ? "user_id,course_id" : "user_id"));
    req.method = "POST";
    req.headers = Object.assign(authHeaders(sess), { Prefer: "resolution=merge-duplicates,return=minimal" });
    req.body = JSON.stringify(COURSE.course
      ? { user_id: sess.userId, course_id: COURSE.course, data: progress }
      : { user_id: sess.userId, data: progress });
    await req.load();
    const code = req.response && req.response.statusCode;
    return !code || code < 300;
  } catch (e) {
    return false;   // offline: iCloud has it, the next run syncs
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- widget

function timeOf(ms) {
  const d = new Date(ms);
  let h = d.getHours();
  const m = String(d.getMinutes()).padStart(2, "0");
  const ap = h >= 12 ? "pm" : "am";
  h = h % 12 || 12;
  return h + ":" + m + ap;
}

/** Plain text of a term, for places that can't render the HTML it may hold
 *  (subscripts in "Real GDP<sub>t</sub>" and the like). */
function plain(html) {
  return String(html || "")
    .replace(/<[^>]+>/g, "")
    .replace(/&amp;/g, "&")
    .replace(/&lt;/g, "<")
    .replace(/&gt;/g, ">")
    .replace(/&[a-z]+;/g, "");
}

function buildWidget(stats) {
  const w = new ListWidget();
  w.backgroundColor = new Color(CREAM);
  w.setPadding(14, 14, 12, 14);
  const big = config.widgetFamily === "medium" || config.widgetFamily === "large";

  const header = w.addStack();
  header.centerAlignContent();
  const title = header.addText(COURSE.code);
  title.font = Font.boldSystemFont(11);
  title.textColor = new Color(BRAND);
  header.addSpacer();
  const owed = stats.due + stats.newLeft;
  if (owed > 0) {
    const badge = header.addText(String(owed));
    badge.font = Font.boldSystemFont(13);
    badge.textColor = new Color(BRAND);
  }

  w.addSpacer(6);

  const card = stats.nextCard;
  if (!card) {
    const t = w.addText("All caught up");
    t.font = Font.semiboldSystemFont(15);
    t.textColor = new Color(INK);
    w.addSpacer(2);
    const s = w.addText(
      stats.upcoming < Infinity ? "Next card at " + timeOf(stats.upcoming) : "Nothing due right now.",
    );
    s.font = Font.systemFont(11);
    s.textColor = new Color(INK);
    s.textOpacity = 0.6;
  } else {
    const week = WEEKS[card.week - 1];
    const wk = w.addText((card.starred ? "★ " : "") + (COURSE.unitTitles ? week.title.toUpperCase() : "WEEK " + card.week));
    wk.font = Font.boldSystemFont(9);
    wk.textColor = new Color(week.color);
    w.addSpacer(2);

    const term = w.addText(plain(card.term));
    term.font = new Font("Georgia-Bold", big ? 24 : 19);
    term.textColor = new Color(INK);
    term.minimumScaleFactor = 0.55;
    term.lineLimit = 3;

    w.addSpacer(4);
    const parts = [];
    if (stats.due) parts.push(stats.due + " due");
    if (stats.newLeft) parts.push(stats.newLeft + " new");
    const hint = w.addText(parts.join(" · ") + " · tap to review");
    hint.font = Font.systemFont(10);
    hint.textColor = new Color(INK);
    hint.textOpacity = 0.6;
  }

  w.addSpacer();

  // Progress toward every card answered confidently at a full day's spacing.
  const bar = w.addStack();
  bar.size = new Size(0, 4);
  bar.cornerRadius = 2;
  bar.backgroundColor = new Color(INK, 0.09);
  const fillWidth = Math.round((big ? 300 : 130) * (stats.solid / stats.total));
  if (fillWidth > 0) {
    const fill = bar.addStack();
    fill.size = new Size(fillWidth, 4);
    fill.cornerRadius = 2;
    fill.backgroundColor = new Color(BRAND);
  }
  bar.addSpacer();
  w.addSpacer(3);
  const solid = w.addText(stats.solid + "/" + stats.total + " solid");
  solid.font = Font.systemFont(9);
  solid.textColor = new Color(INK);
  solid.textOpacity = 0.45;

  // Tapping anywhere runs this same script, which then falls into review mode.
  w.url = "scriptable:///run?scriptName=" + encodeURIComponent(Script.name());
  if (stats.due === 0 && stats.newLeft === 0 && stats.upcoming < Infinity) {
    w.refreshAfterDate = new Date(stats.upcoming + 60 * 1000);
  }
  return w;
}

// -------------------------------------------------------- review session

/** What the page needs about one card: the card itself plus the terms and
 *  one-line definitions of the cards it connects to, so a connection can be
 *  peeked at without leaving the session. */
function forPage(card) {
  const links = (card.connections || [])
    .map((id) => BY_ID[id])
    .filter(Boolean)
    .map((c) => ({ id: c.id, term: c.term, simple: c.simple, formula: c.formula || "", week: c.week }));
  return { ...card, links };
}

function reviewHTML(cards, mode) {
  const practice = mode === "cram";
  // Cards go in as JSON rather than templated into markup, and every "<" is
  // escaped so nothing in a card can close the script tag early.
  const json = (v) => JSON.stringify(v).replace(/</g, "\\u003c");
  const fill = (src, marker, value) => src.split(marker).join(value);

  let js = PAGE.js;
  js = fill(js, "/*__CARDS__*/[]", json(cards.map(forPage)));
  js = fill(js, "/*__WEEKS__*/[]", json(WEEKS));
  js = fill(js, "/*__PRACTICE__*/false", practice ? "true" : "false");
  if (COURSE.unitTitles) js = fill(js, "/*__UI__*/{}", json({ unitTitles: true }));
  let body = PAGE.body;
  body = fill(body, "__QUIT__", practice ? "Cram \u00b7 nothing is rescheduled" : "Swipe down when you\u2019re done");
  body = fill(body, "__DONE_ACTIONS__", '<p style="opacity:.45;font-size:13px">Swipe down to save and close.</p>');

  return '<!doctype html>\n<html><head><meta charset="utf-8">\n' +
    '<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1,maximum-scale=1,user-scalable=no,viewport-fit=cover">\n' +
    "<style>\n" + PAGE.css + "</style></head>\n<body>\n" + body +
    "\n<script>\n" + GRAPH_SRC.replace(/<\/script/gi, "<\\/script") + "\n</script>\n<script>\n" + js + "</script>\n</body></html>";
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------- session run

async function pick(title, message, options) {
  const a = new Alert();
  a.title = title;
  if (message) a.message = message;
  for (const o of options) a.addAction(o);
  a.addCancelAction("Cancel");
  return a.presentAlert();
}

async function tell(title, message) {
  const a = new Alert();
  a.title = title;
  a.message = message;
  a.addAction("OK");
  await a.presentAlert();
}

/** The start menu. Returns {cards, mode} or null if cancelled. */
async function chooseSession(progress, sess) {
  const now = Date.now();
  const st = summary(CARDS, progress, now);
  const starred = (c) => c.starred;
  const tested = buildQueue(CARDS, progress, now, starred, Infinity);
  const testedCount = tested.due.length + tested.fresh.length;

  const options = [];
  const actions = [];
  if (st.due + st.newLeft > 0) {
    const parts = [];
    if (st.due) parts.push(st.due + " due");
    if (st.newLeft) parts.push(st.newLeft + " new");
    options.push("Review " + parts.join(" + "));
    actions.push("review");
  }
  if (testedCount > 0) {
    options.push("★ Tested cards only (" + testedCount + ")");
    actions.push("tested");
  }
  options.push("One " + COURSE.unitWord.toLowerCase() + "…");
  actions.push("week");
  options.push("Cram all " + CARDS.length + " · no scheduling");
  actions.push("cram");
  options.push("Progress & reset…");
  actions.push("stats");
  if (!sess) {
    options.push("Sign in to sync with the website…");
    actions.push("signin");
  }

  let message = st.solid + " of " + st.total + " solid" + (sess ? " · synced" : "");
  if (st.due + st.newLeft === 0) {
    message = "All caught up" +
      (st.upcoming < Infinity ? " · next card at " + timeOf(st.upcoming) : "") +
      "\n" + message;
  }
  const choice = await pick(COURSE.title, message, options);
  if (choice === -1) return null;
  const act = actions[choice];

  if (act === "signin") return { signin: true };
  if (act === "review") {
    const q = buildQueue(CARDS, progress, now, null, st.newLeft);
    return { cards: interleave(q.due, q.fresh), mode: "review" };
  }
  if (act === "tested") {
    return { cards: interleave(tested.due, tested.fresh), mode: "review" };
  }
  if (act === "week") {
    const labels = WEEKS.map((w, k) => {
      const q = buildQueue(CARDS, progress, now, (c) => c.week === k + 1, Infinity);
      const n = q.due.length + q.fresh.length;
      return COURSE.unitWord + " " + (k + 1) + ": " + w.title + (n ? " (" + n + ")" : " ✓");
    });
    const unit = COURSE.unitWord.toLowerCase();
    const k = await pick("Which " + unit + "?", "Due and new cards from that " + unit + " only.", labels);
    if (k === -1) return null;
    const q = buildQueue(CARDS, progress, now, (c) => c.week === k + 1, Infinity);
    if (q.due.length + q.fresh.length === 0) {
      const again = await pick(
        COURSE.unitWord + " " + (k + 1) + " is caught up",
        "Nothing in it is due. Cram it anyway? That won't change its schedule.",
        ["Cram " + unit + " " + (k + 1)],
      );
      if (again === -1) return null;
      return { cards: shuffle(CARDS.filter((c) => c.week === k + 1)), mode: "cram" };
    }
    return { cards: interleave(q.due, q.fresh), mode: "review" };
  }
  if (act === "cram") {
    return { cards: shuffle(CARDS.slice()), mode: "cram" };
  }
  if (act === "stats") {
    await showStats(progress);
    return null;
  }
  return null;
}

async function showStats(progress) {
  const now = Date.now();
  const st = summary(CARDS, progress, now);
  const lines = WEEKS.map((w, k) => {
    const cs = CARDS.filter((c) => c.week === k + 1);
    const seen = cs.filter((c) => progress.cards[c.id]).length;
    const solid = cs.filter((c) => (progress.cards[c.id] || {}).solid).length;
    return COURSE.unitWord + " " + (k + 1) + ": " + seen + "/" + cs.length + " seen · " + solid + " solid";
  });
  // The cards missed most, worth a look in the notes.
  const hard = CARDS
    .map((c) => ({ c, s: progress.cards[c.id] }))
    .filter((x) => x.s && x.s.lapses > 0)
    .sort((a, b) => b.s.lapses - a.s.lapses)
    .slice(0, 5)
    .map((x) => "• " + plain(x.c.term) + " (" + x.s.lapses + "×)");
  const msg = lines.join("\n") +
    "\n\n" + st.unseen + " never seen · " + st.due + " due now" +
    (hard.length ? "\n\nMissed most:\n" + hard.join("\n") : "");

  const a = new Alert();
  a.title = st.solid + " of " + st.total + " solid";
  a.message = msg;
  a.addAction("OK");
  a.addDestructiveAction("Reset all progress");
  const choice = await a.presentAlert();
  if (choice === 1) {
    const sure = new Alert();
    sure.title = "Reset everything?";
    sure.message = "Every card goes back to new. This can't be undone.";
    sure.addDestructiveAction("Reset");
    sure.addCancelAction("Cancel");
    if ((await sure.presentAlert()) === 0) {
      const fresh = { cards: {}, days: progress.days || {}, resetAt: Date.now() };
      for (const k in progress) delete progress[k];
      Object.assign(progress, fresh);
      saveProgress(progress);
      await tell("Reset", "All " + CARDS.length + " cards are new again.");
    }
  }
}

async function runReview() {
  const progress = await loadProgress();
  let sess = await signIn(false);
  if (sess && !(await syncNow(sess, progress))) sess = null;
  let session = await chooseSession(progress, sess);
  while (session && session.signin) {
    sess = await signIn(true);
    if (sess && (await syncNow(sess, progress))) await tell("Synced", "This phone and the website now share progress.");
    else { sess = null; await tell("Couldn't sign in", "Check the email and password (the ones you use for Wazn) and your connection."); }
    session = await chooseSession(progress, sess);
  }
  if (!session || !session.cards.length) { if (sess) await syncNow(sess, progress); return; }
  const practice = session.mode === "cram";

  // The page shows "Confident · in N hours" from each card's ladder step.
  const cards = session.cards.map((c) => {
    const s = progress.cards[c.id];
    return { ...c, step: s ? s.step : 0 };
  });

  const applied = new Set();
  function apply(seq, id, rating, t) {
    if (practice || applied.has(seq) || !BY_ID[id]) return;
    if (!["missed", "shaky", "confident"].includes(rating)) return;
    progress.cards[id] = schedule(progress.cards[id], rating, t || Date.now());
    progress.days = progress.days || {};
    const day = isoDay(t || Date.now());
    progress.days[day] = (progress.days[day] || 0) + 1;
    applied.add(seq);
    try {
      saveProgress(progress);
    } catch (e) {
      applied.delete(seq);   // the pass after dismissal will try again
    }
  }

  const wv = new WebView();
  await wv.loadHTML(reviewHTML(cards, session.mode));

  // The page can't call into Scriptable mid-session, so it navigates to an
  // econ381:// URL and this handler intercepts it. Returning false blocks
  // the navigation and leaves the session on screen untouched.
  wv.shouldAllowRequest = (request) => {
    const url = String((request && request.url) || "");
    if (!url.startsWith("econ381://")) return true;
    if (url.startsWith("econ381://grade")) {
      const get = (k) => {
        const m = url.match(new RegExp("[?&]" + k + "=([^&]+)"));
        return m ? decodeURIComponent(m[1]) : null;
      };
      apply(Number(get("seq")), get("id"), get("rating"), Number(get("t")));
    }
    return false;
  };

  // present() resolves when the sheet is dismissed; the page is still alive
  // and its list of grades can be read back. Anything the live saves missed
  // is applied now, in order, so a session abandoned halfway still counts.
  await wv.present(true);

  let graded = [];
  try {
    const raw = await wv.evaluateJavaScript("JSON.stringify(results)", false);
    const parsed = JSON.parse(raw || "[]");
    if (Array.isArray(parsed)) graded = parsed;
  } catch (e) {
    /* nothing graded, or the page was gone */
  }
  graded.forEach((r, seq) => apply(seq, r.id, r.rating, r.t));
  if (sess && !practice) await syncNow(sess, progress);
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ main

if (config.runsInWidget) {
  const progress = await loadProgress();
  try {
    const sess = await signIn(false);
    if (sess) { mergeProgress(progress, await pullRemote(sess)); saveProgress(progress); }
  } catch (e) { /* offline: the iCloud copy is fine */ }
  Script.setWidget(buildWidget(summary(CARDS, progress, Date.now())));
  Script.complete();
} else {
  await runReview();
  Script.complete();
}
